images:
  - file: Vergina2.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Vergina2.jpg/250px-Vergina2.jpg
    width: 300
    height: 201
    caption: The entrance to one of the royal tombs at Vergina, a UNESCO World
      Heritage Site
  - file: Oktadrachm_of_Alexander_I_498_–_454_BCE.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Oktadrachm_of_Alexander_I_498_%E2%80%93_454_BCE.jpg/250px-Oktadrachm_of_Alexander_I_498_%E2%80%93_454_BCE.jpg
    width: 500
    height: 229
    caption: A silver octadrachm of Alexander I of Macedon (r. 498–454 BC), minted
      c. 465–460 BC, showing an equestrian figure wearing a chlamys (short
      cloak) and petasos (head cap) while holding two spears and leading a horse
  - file: Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg/330px-Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg.png
    width: 993
    height: 794
    caption: Macedon (orange) during the Peloponnesian War around 431 BC, with
      Athens and the Delian League (yellow), Sparta and Peloponnesian League
      (red), independent states (blue), and the Persian Achaemenid Empire
      (purple)
  - file: Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg/250px-Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg
    width: 500
    height: 232
    caption: A Macedonian didrachm minted during the reign of Archelaus I of Macedon
      (r. 413–399 BC)
  - file: Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg/250px-Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg
    width: 500
    height: 252
    caption: A silver stater of Amyntas III of Macedon (r. 393–370 BC)
  - file: Expansion_of_Macedon_(English).svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Expansion_of_Macedon_%28English%29.svg/330px-Expansion_of_Macedon_%28English%29.svg.png
    width: 992
    height: 794
    caption: Map of the Kingdom of Macedon at the death of Philip II in 336 BC
      (light blue), with the original territory that existed in 431 BC (red
      outline), and dependent states (yellow)
  - file: MacedonEmpire.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/MacedonEmpire.jpg/330px-MacedonEmpire.jpg
    width: 1990
    height: 951
    caption: Alexander's empire and his route
  - file: Stag_hunt_mosaic,_Pella.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Stag_hunt_mosaic%2C_Pella.jpg/250px-Stag_hunt_mosaic%2C_Pella.jpg
    width: 1533
    height: 964
    caption: The Stag Hunt Mosaic, c. 300 BC, from Pella; the figure on the right is
      possibly Alexander the Great due to the date of the mosaic along with the
      depicted upsweep of his centrally-parted hair (anastole); the figure on
      the left wielding a double-edged axe (associated with Hephaistos) is
      perhaps Hephaestion, one of Alexander's loyal companions.
  - file: Diadochi_LA.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Diadochi_LA.svg/330px-Diadochi_LA.svg.png
    width: 2000
    height: 961
    caption: Kingdoms of the Diadochi c. 301 BC, after the Battle of Ipsus Kingdom
      of Ptolemy I Soter Kingdom of Cassander Kingdom of Lysimachus Kingdom of
      Seleucus I Nicator Epirus Other Carthage Roman Republic Greek States
  - file: Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg/250px-Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg
    width: 600
    height: 290
    caption: A golden stater of Philip III Arrhidaeus (r. 323–317 BC) bearing images
      of Athena (left) and Nike (right)
  - file: Ancient_Mieza,_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia,_Tomb_of_Lyson_and_Kallikles_a72b9b998c2e98a1390dbae9e032ea1c.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_Tomb_of_Lyson_and_Kallikles_a72b9b998c2e98a1390dbae9e032ea1c.jpg/250px-Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_Tomb_of_Lyson_and_Kallikles_a72b9b998c2e98a1390dbae9e032ea1c.jpg
    width: 564
    height: 359
    caption: Paintings of Hellenistic-era military arms and armor from a tomb in
      ancient Mieza (modern-day Lefkadia), Imathia, Central Macedonia, Greece,
      2nd century BC
  - file: Apollon_Tempel_im_antiken_Korinth.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Apollon_Tempel_im_antiken_Korinth.jpg/250px-Apollon_Tempel_im_antiken_Korinth.jpg
    width: 2004
    height: 1338
    caption: The Temple of Apollo at Corinth, built c. 540 BC, with the Acrocorinth
      (i.e. the acropolis of Corinth that once held a Macedonian garrison) seen
      in the background
  - file: Tetradrachm,_229-221,_Antigonos_Doson.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Tetradrachm%2C_229-221%2C_Antigonos_Doson.jpg/250px-Tetradrachm%2C_229-221%2C_Antigonos_Doson.jpg
    width: 800
    height: 376
    caption: A tetradrachm minted during the reign of Antigonus III Doson (r.
      229–221 BC), possibly at Amphipolis, bearing the portrait image of
      Poseidon on the obverse and on the reverse a scene depicting Apollo
      sitting on the prow of a ship
  - file: Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png/330px-Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png
    width: 750
    height: 599
    caption: The Kingdom of Macedonia (orange) under Philip V (r. 221–179 BC), with
      Macedonian dependent states (dark yellow), the Seleucid Empire (bright
      yellow), Roman protectorates (dark green), the Kingdom of Pergamon (light
      green), independent states (light purple), and possessions of the
      Ptolemaic Empire (violet purple)
  - file: Philip_V._221-179_BCE.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Philip_V._221-179_BCE.jpg/250px-Philip_V._221-179_BCE.jpg
    width: 500
    height: 211
    caption: A tetradrachm of Philip V of Macedon (r. 221–179 BC), with the king's
      portrait on the obverse and Athena Alkidemos brandishing a thunderbolt on
      the reverse
  - file: Eumene_II,_fondatore_della_biblioteca_di_pergamo,_copia_romana_(50_dc_ca)_da_orig,._ellenistico_su_busto_moderno,_MANN_02.JPG
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Eumene_II%2C_fondatore_della_biblioteca_di_pergamo%2C_copia_romana_%2850_dc_ca%29_da_orig%2C._ellenistico_su_busto_moderno%2C_MANN_02.JPG/250px-Eumene_II%2C_fondatore_della_biblioteca_di_pergamo%2C_copia_romana_%2850_dc_ca%29_da_orig%2C._ellenistico_su_busto_moderno%2C_MANN_02.JPG
    width: 2046
    height: 3510
    caption: Bronze bust of Eumenes II of Pergamon, a Roman copy of a Hellenistic
      Greek original, from the Villa of the Papyri in Herculaneum
  - file: Phillip_Museum.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Golden_larnax_and_wreath_of_Philip_II_of_Macedon_at_the_Vergina_museum.jpg/250px-Golden_larnax_and_wreath_of_Philip_II_of_Macedon_at_the_Vergina_museum.jpg
    width: 628
    height: 414
    caption: The Vergina Sun, the 16-ray star covering the royal burial larnax of
      Philip II of Macedon (r. 359–336 BC), discovered in the tomb of Vergina,
      formerly ancient Aigai
  - file: Hades_abducting_Persephone.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Hades_abducting_Persephone.jpg/250px-Hades_abducting_Persephone.jpg
    width: 2994
    height: 1091
    caption: Hades abducting Persephone, fresco in the small Macedonian royal tomb
      at Vergina, Macedonia, Greece, c. 340 BC
  - file: Thueros_affresco.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Thueros_affresco.jpg/250px-Thueros_affresco.jpg
    width: 2376
    height: 3564
    caption: Fresco of an ancient Macedonian soldier (thorakites) wearing chainmail
      armor and bearing a thureos shield, 3rd century BC, İstanbul Archaeology
      Museums
  - file: The_Abduction_of_Persephone_by_Pluto,_Amphipolis.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/The_Abduction_of_Persephone_by_Pluto%2C_Amphipolis.jpg/250px-The_Abduction_of_Persephone_by_Pluto%2C_Amphipolis.jpg
    width: 995
    height: 720
    caption: A mosaic of the Kasta Tomb in Amphipolis depicting the abduction of
      Persephone by Pluto, 4th century BC
  - file: Lion_of_Amphipolis_BW_2017-10-05_09-38-25.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Lion_of_Amphipolis_BW_2017-10-05_09-38-25.jpg/250px-Lion_of_Amphipolis_BW_2017-10-05_09-38-25.jpg
    width: 3327
    height: 5335
    caption: The Lion of Amphipolis in Amphipolis, northern Greece, a 4th-century BC
      marble tomb sculpture erected in honor of Laomedon of Mytilene, a general
      who served under Alexander the Great
  - file: Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg/250px-Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg
    width: 850
    height: 384
    caption: Alexander (left), wearing a kausia and fighting an Asiatic lion with
      his friend Craterus (detail); late 4th-century BC mosaic, Pella Museum.
  - file: Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg/250px-Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg
    width: 1680
    height: 2241
    caption: Portrait bust of Aristotle, an Imperial Roman (1st or 2nd century AD)
      copy of a lost bronze sculpture made by Lysippos
  - file: Hades_and_Persephone,_Vergina.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Hades_and_Persephone%2C_Vergina.jpg/250px-Hades_and_Persephone%2C_Vergina.jpg
    width: 3032
    height: 1740
    caption: A fresco showing Hades and Persephone riding in a chariot, from the
      tomb of Queen Eurydice I of Macedon at Vergina, Greece, 4th century BC
  - file: Banquet,_tombe_d'Agios_Athanasios.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Banquet%2C_tombe_d%27Agios_Athanasios.jpg/250px-Banquet%2C_tombe_d%27Agios_Athanasios.jpg
    width: 3096
    height: 898
    caption: A banquet scene from a Macedonian tomb of Agios Athanasios,
      Thessaloniki, 4th century BC; shown are six men reclining on couches, with
      food arranged on nearby tables, a male servant in attendance, and female
      musicians providing entertainment.
  - file: 20100913_Ancient_Theater_Marwneia_Rhodope_Greece_panoramic_3.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/20100913_Ancient_Theater_Marwneia_Rhodope_Greece_panoramic_3.jpg/250px-20100913_Ancient_Theater_Marwneia_Rhodope_Greece_panoramic_3.jpg
    width: 4177
    height: 1678
    caption: Ruins of the ancient theatre in Maroneia, Rhodope, East Macedonia and
      Thrace, Greece
  - file: Mints_of_Alexander_the_Great_1.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Mints_of_Alexander_the_Great_1.jpg/250px-Mints_of_Alexander_the_Great_1.jpg
    width: 1809
    height: 2382
    caption: Tetradrachms (above) and drachms (below) issued during the reign of
      Alexander the Great, now in the Numismatic Museum of Athens
  - file: Battle_of_Issos_MAN_Napoli_Inv10020_n01.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Battle_of_Issos_MAN_Napoli_Inv10020_n01.jpg/250px-Battle_of_Issos_MAN_Napoli_Inv10020_n01.jpg
    width: 3625
    height: 1700
    caption: The Alexander Mosaic, a Roman mosaic from Pompeii, Italy, c. 100 BC
