source:
  site: Wikipedia
  lang: en
  title: Macedonia (ancient kingdom)
  url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_(ancient_kingdom)
  page-id: 42012
  revision: "1369660498"
  modified: 2026-08-16T09:34:55Z
  retrieved: 2026-09-01
  license: CC BY-SA 4.0
  license-url: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
  attribution: This document contains text from the Wikipedia article "Macedonia
    (ancient kingdom)" (revision 1369660498), by Wikipedia contributors, used
    under CC BY-SA 4.0.
+++
!!Macedonia!!
([[ /ˌmæsɪˈdoʊniə/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]
//MASS-ih-DOH-nee-ə//; Greek: Μακεδονία, //Makedonía//), also called
!!Macedon!!
([[ /ˈmæsɪdɒn/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]
//MASS-ih-don//), was an [[ ancient | classical-antiquity ]]
[[ kingdom | monarchy ]] on the periphery of [[ Archaic | archaic-greece ]]
and [[ Classical | classical-greece ]] Greece,[[ ^8 ]] which later became the
dominant state of [[ Hellenistic Greece ]].[[ ^9 ]] The kingdom was founded
and initially ruled by the royal [[ Argead dynasty ]], which was followed by
the [[ Antipatrid | antipatrid-dynasty ]] and
[[ Antigonid | antigonid-dynasty ]] dynasties. Home to the
[[ ancient Macedonians ]], the earliest kingdom was centred on the
northeastern part of the [[ Greek peninsula ]],[[ ^10 ]] and bordered by
[[ Epirus | epirus-ancient-state ]] to the southwest, [[ Illyria ]] to the
northwest, [[ Paeonia | paeonia-kingdom ]] to the north,
[[ Thrace | ancient-thrace ]] to the east and
[[ Thessaly | ancient-thessaly ]] to the south.

Before the 4th century BC, Macedonia was a small kingdom centered in
[[ Aegae | aegae-macedonia ]], outside of the area dominated by the
[[ city-states | polis ]] of [[ Athens | classical-athens ]], [[ Sparta ]],
and [[ Thebes | classical-thebes ]], and
[[ briefly subordinate | achaemenid-macedonia ]] to the
[[ Achaemenid Empire ]]. During the reign of king
[[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] (359–336 BC), Macedonia with its
capital in [[ Pella ]], [[ subdued | rise-of-macedon ]] mainland Greece,
Paeonia, and the Thracian [[ Odrysian kingdom ]] through conquest and
diplomacy. With a reformed [[ army | ancient-macedonian-army ]] containing
[[ phalanxes | macedonian-phalanx ]] wielding the //[[ sarissa ]]// pike,
Philip II defeated the old powers of [[ Athens | second-athenian-league ]] and
[[ Thebes | theban-hegemony ]] in the
[[ Battle of Chaeronea | battle-of-chaeronea-338-bc ]] in 338 BC. Philip II's
son [[ Alexander the Great ]], leading a
[[ federation of Greek states | league-of-corinth ]] founded by his father,
accomplished the objective of commanding the whole of Greece when he
[[ destroyed Thebes | battle-of-thebes ]] after the city revolted. During
Alexander's subsequent
[[ campaign of conquest | chronology-of-the-expedition-of-alexander-the-great-into-asia ]],
he [[ overthrew | wars-of-alexander-the-great ]] the Achaemenid Empire and
conquered territory that stretched as far as the [[ Indus River ]]. For a
brief period, his !!Macedonian Empire!! was the most powerful in the world –
the definitive [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]] state, inaugurating the
transition to a new period of [[ Ancient Greek civilization ]].
[[ Greek arts | hellenistic-art ]] and
[[ literature | hellenistic-literature ]] flourished in the new conquered
lands and advances in [[ philosophy | ancient-greek-philosophy ]],
[[ engineering | ancient-greek-engineering ]], and
[[ science | ancient-greek-science ]] spread across the empire and beyond. Of
particular importance were the contributions of [[ Aristotle ]], tutor to
Alexander, [[ whose writings | aristotelianism ]] became a keystone of
[[ Western philosophy ]].

After [[ Alexander's death | death-of-alexander-the-great ]] in 323 BC, the
ensuing [[ wars of the Diadochi ]], and the partitioning of Alexander's
short-lived empire, Macedonia remained a Greek cultural and political centre
in the [[ Mediterranean ]] region along with [[ Ptolemaic Egypt ]], the
[[ Seleucid Empire ]], and the [[ Attalid kingdom ]]. Important cities
included its capital [[ Pella ]], [[ Dion | dion-pieria ]], [[ Beroea ]],
[[ Pydna ]], and [[ Amphipolis ]], while new cities were founded, such as
[[ Thessalonica ]] by the usurper [[ Cassander ]] (named after his wife
[[ Thessalonike of Macedon ]]).[[ ^11 ]] Macedonia's decline began with the
[[ Second Macedonian War ]] and [[ the rise | rise-of-rome ]] of
[[ Rome | ancient-rome ]] as the leading Mediterranean power. At the end of
the [[ Third Macedonian War ]] in 168 BC,
[[ the Macedonian monarchy was abolished | greece-in-the-roman-era ]] and
replaced by Roman [[ client states | client-state ]]. A short-lived revival of
the monarchy during the [[ Fourth Macedonian War ]] in 150–148 BC ended with
the establishment of the [[ Roman province ]] of
[[ Macedonia | macedonia-roman-province ]].

The Macedonian kings, who wielded [[ absolute power | absolute-monarchy ]] and
commanded [[ state resources | state-ownership ]] such as gold and silver,
facilitated mining operations to [[ mint | mint-facility ]]
[[ currency | ancient-greek-coinage ]], finance
[[ their armies | antigonid-macedonian-army ]] and, by the reign of Philip II,
a Macedonian navy. Unlike the other //[[ diadochi ]]//
[[ successor states | successor-state ]], the [[ imperial cult ]] fostered by
Alexander was never adopted in Macedonia, yet Macedonian rulers nevertheless
assumed roles as [[ high priests | high-priest ]] of the kingdom and leading
patrons of domestic and international [[ cults | ancient-greek-religion ]] of
the [[ Hellenistic religion ]]. The authority of Macedonian kings was
theoretically limited by the institution of the army, while
[[ a few municipalities | government-of-macedonia-ancient-kingdom ]] within
the [[ Macedonian commonwealth | koinon-of-macedonians ]] enjoyed a high
degree of autonomy and even had democratic governments with popular
assemblies.

== Etymology

The name Macedonia (Greek: Μακεδονία, //Makedonía//) comes from the
[[ ethnonym ]] Μακεδόνες (//Makedónes//), which itself is derived from the
[[ ancient Greek ]] adjective [[ μακεδνός | makednos ]] (//makednós//),
meaning 'tall, slim', also the name of a people related to the [[ Dorians ]]
([[ Herodotus ]]), and possibly descriptive of
[[ Ancient Macedonians ]].[[ ^12 ]] It is most likely [[ cognate ]] with the
adjective μακρός (//makrós//), meaning 'long' or 'tall' in
[[ Ancient Greek ]].[[ ^12 ]] The name is believed to have originally meant
either 'highlanders', 'the tall ones', or 'high grown men'.[[ ^1 ]] Linguist
[[ Robert S. P. Beekes ]] claims that both terms are of
[[ Pre-Greek substrate ]] origin and cannot be explained in terms of
[[ Indo-European | indo-european-languages ]] morphology;[[ ^13 ]] however
Filip De Decker rejects Beekesʼ arguments as insufficient.[[ ^14 ]] The
shorter English name variant //Macedon// developed in Middle English, based on
a borrowing from the French form of the name, //Macédoine//.[[ ^15 ]]

== History

=== Early history and legend

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Vergina2.jpg/250px-Vergina2.jpg" height="168" width="250"

  <figcaption>The entrance to one of the royal tombs at [[ Vergina ]], a [[ UNESCO World Heritage Site ]]

The [[ Classical | classical-greece ]]
[[ Greek historians | greek-historiography ]] [[ Herodotus ]] and
[[ Thucydides ]] reported the [[ legend | origin-myth ]] that the
[[ Macedonian kings | list-of-macedonian-kings ]] of the [[ Argead dynasty ]]
were descendants of [[ Temenus ]], king of [[ Argos | argos-peloponnese ]],
and could therefore claim the mythical [[ Heracles ]] as one of their
[[ ancestors | ancestor ]] as well as
[[ a direct lineage | family-tree-of-the-greek-gods ]] from [[ Zeus ]], chief
god of the [[ Greek pantheon | greek-mythology ]].[[ ^16 ]] Contradictory
legends state that either [[ Perdiccas I of Macedon ]] or
[[ Caranus of Macedon ]] were the founders of the Argead dynasty, with either
five or eight kings before Amyntas I.[[ ^17 ]] The assertion that the Argeads
descended from Temenus was accepted by the //[[ Hellanodikai ]]// authorities
of the [[ Ancient Olympic Games ]], permitting [[ Alexander I of Macedon ]]
(r. 498–454 BC) to enter the competitions owing to his perceived Greek
heritage.[[ ^18 ]] Little is known about the kingdom before the reign of
Alexander I's father [[ Amyntas I of Macedon ]] (r. 547–498 BC) during the
[[ Archaic period | archaic-greece ]].[[ ^19 ]]

The [[ kingdom of Macedonia | history-of-macedonia-ancient-kingdom ]] was
initially situated along the [[ Haliacmon ]] and [[ Loudias ]] rivers in
[[ Lower Macedonia ]], north of [[ Mount Olympus ]], east of the
[[ Vermio | vermio-mountains ]] and [[ Pierian | pierian-mountains ]] mountain
ranges, stretching along the coast of the [[ Thermaic Gulf ]]. Historian
[[ Robert Malcolm Errington ]] suggests that one of the earliest Argead kings
established [[ Aigai | aegae-macedonia ]] (modern [[ Vergina ]]) as their
capital in the mid-7th century BC.[[ ^20 ]] Before the 4th century BC, the
kingdom covered a region corresponding roughly to the
[[ western | western-macedonia ]] and [[ central | central-macedonia ]] parts
of the [[ region of Macedonia | macedonia-greece ]] in modern
[[ Greece ]].[[ ^21 ]] It gradually expanded into the regions of
[[ Emathia | emathia-macedonia ]], [[ Pieria | pieria-regional-unit ]],
[[ Bottiaea ]], [[ Almopia ]], [[ Mygdonia ]], [[ Crestonia ]], and
[[ Bisaltia ]], which were inhabited by various peoples such as
[[ Thracians ]] and [[ Phrygians ]], and into [[ Eordaea ]] and the rest of
[[ Upper Macedonia ]], inhabited by the Greek tribes of [[ Lyncestae ]],
[[ Orestae ]], and [[ Elimiotae ]].[[ ^2 ]] Macedonia's non-Greek neighbours
included Thracians, inhabiting territories to the northeast, [[ Illyrians ]]
to the northwest, and [[ Paeonians ]] to the north, while the lands of
[[ Thessaly ]] to the south and [[ Epirus ]] to the west were inhabited by
Greeks with similar cultures to that of the Macedonians.[[ ^22 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Oktadrachm_of_Alexander_I_498_%E2%80%93_454_BCE.jpg/250px-Oktadrachm_of_Alexander_I_498_%E2%80%93_454_BCE.jpg" height="115" width="250"

  <figcaption>A silver //[[ octadrachm | drachm ]]// of [[ Alexander I of Macedon ]] (r. 498–454 BC), [[ minted | mint-facility ]] c. 465–460 BC, showing an [[ equestrian | equestrianism ]] figure wearing a //[[ chlamys ]]// (short cloak) and //[[ petasos ]]// (head cap) while holding two spears and leading a horse

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_with_petasos_or_kausia_soldier_circa_480_BCE_cleaned_up.jpg/120px-Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_with_petasos_or_kausia_soldier_circa_480_BCE_cleaned_up.jpg" height="201" width="106"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_with_petasos_or_kausia.jpg/250px-Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_with_petasos_or_kausia.jpg" height="201" width="150"

The "Ionians with shield-hats" ([[ Old Persian cuneiform ]]:
[[ 𐎹𐎢𐎴𐎠𐏐𐎫𐎣𐎲𐎼𐎠 | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/𐎹𐎢𐎴𐎠%20𐏐%20𐎫𐎣𐎲𐎼𐎠 ]],
//Yaunā takabarā//)[[ ^23 ]] depicted on the tomb of [[ Xerxes I ]] at
[[ Naqsh-e Rustam ]], were probably Macedonian soldiers in the service of the
[[ Achaemenid army ]], wearing the [[ petasos ]] or [[ kausia ]], c.480
BC.[[ ^24 ]]

A year after [[ Darius I of Persia ]] (r. 522–486 BC) launched
[[ an invasion | european-scythian-campaign-of-darius-i ]] into Europe against
the [[ Scythians ]], [[ Paeonians ]], [[ Thracians | thrace ]], and several
[[ Greek | ancient-greece ]] city-states in coastal Thrace, the Persian
general [[ Megabazus ]] used diplomacy to convince Amyntas I to submit as a
[[ vassal | vassal-state ]] of the [[ Achaemenid Empire ]], ushering in the
period of [[ Achaemenid Macedonia ]].[[ ^3 ]] Achaemenid Persian
[[ hegemony ]] over Macedonia was briefly interrupted by the
[[ Ionian Revolt ]] (499–493 BC), yet the Persian general
[[ Mardonius | mardonius-general ]] brought it back under Achaemenid
[[ suzerainty ]].[[ ^25 ]]

Although Macedonia enjoyed a large degree of [[ autonomy ]] and was never made
a [[ satrapy | satrap ]] (i.e. province) of the Achaemenid Empire, it was
expected to provide troops for the [[ Achaemenid army ]].[[ ^26 ]] Alexander I
provided Macedonian military support to [[ Xerxes I ]] (r. 486–465 BC) during
the [[ Second Persian invasion of Greece ]] in 480–479 BC, and Macedonian
soldiers fought on the side of the Persians at the 479 BC
[[ Battle of Platea ]].[[ ^27 ]] Following the
[[ Greek victory at Salamis | battle-of-salamis ]] in 480 BC, Alexander I was
employed as an Achaemenid diplomat to propose a peace treaty and alliance with
[[ Athens | classical-athens ]], an offer that was rejected.[[ ^28 ]] Soon
afterwards, the Achaemenid forces were
[[ forced to withdraw from mainland Europe | wars-of-the-delian-league ]],
marking the end of Persian control over Macedonia.[[ ^29 ]]

=== Involvement in the Classical Greek world

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg/330px-Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg.png" height="240" width="300"

  <figcaption>Macedon (orange) during the [[ Peloponnesian War ]] around 431 BC, with [[ Athens | classical-athens ]] and the [[ Delian League ]] (yellow), [[ Sparta ]] and [[ Peloponnesian League ]] (red), independent states (blue), and the Persian [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] (purple)

Although initially a Persian vassal, Alexander I of Macedon fostered friendly
diplomatic relations with his former Greek enemies, the Athenian and
[[ Spartan | sparta ]]-led coalition of Greek city-states.[[ ^30 ]] His
successor [[ Perdiccas II | perdiccas-ii-of-macedon ]] (r. 454–413 BC) led the
Macedonians to war in four separate conflicts against Athens, leader of the
[[ Delian League ]], while incursions by the Thracian ruler [[ Sitalces ]] of
the [[ Odrysian kingdom ]] threatened Macedonia's [[ territorial integrity ]]
in the northeast.[[ ^31 ]] The Athenian statesman [[ Pericles ]] promoted
colonization of the [[ Strymon River ]] near the Kingdom of Macedonia, where
the colonial city of [[ Amphipolis ]] was founded in 437/436 BC so that it
could provide Athens with a steady supply of silver and gold as well as
[[ timber ]] and [[ pitch | pitch-resin ]] to support the
[[ Athenian navy ]].[[ ^32 ]] Initially Perdiccas II did not take any action
and might have even welcomed the Athenians, as the Thracians were foes to both
of them.[[ ^33 ]] This changed due to an Athenian alliance with a brother and
cousin of Perdiccas II who had rebelled against him.[[ ^33 ]] Thus, two
separate wars were fought against Athens between 433 and 431 BC.[[ ^33 ]] The
Macedonian king retaliated by promoting the rebellion of Athens' allies in
[[ Chalcidice ]] and subsequently won over the strategic city of
[[ Potidaea ]].[[ ^34 ]] After capturing the Macedonian cities [[ Therma ]]
and [[ Beroea | veria ]], Athens besieged Potidaea but failed to overcome it;
Therma was returned to Macedonia and much of Chalcidice to Athens in a
[[ peace treaty ]] brokered by Sitalces, who provided Athens with military aid
in exchange for acquiring new Thracian allies.[[ ^35 ]]

Perdiccas II sided [[ with Sparta | peloponnesian-league ]] in the
[[ Peloponnesian War ]] (431–404 BC) between Athens and Sparta, and in 429 BC
Athens retaliated by persuading Sitalces to invade Macedonia, but he was
forced to retreat owing to a shortage of provisions in winter.[[ ^36 ]] In 424
BC, [[ Arrhabaeus ]], a local ruler of [[ Lynkestis ]] in Upper Macedonia,
rebelled against his [[ overlord | suzerain ]] Perdiccas, and the Spartans
agreed to help in putting down the revolt.[[ ^37 ]] At the
[[ Battle of Lyncestis ]] the Macedonians panicked and fled before the
fighting began, enraging the Spartan general [[ Brasidas ]], whose soldiers
looted the unattended Macedonian [[ baggage train ]].[[ ^38 ]] Perdiccas then
changed sides and supported Athens, and he was able to put down Arrhabaeus's
revolt.[[ ^39 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg/250px-Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg" height="116" width="250"

  <figcaption>A Macedonian [[ didrachm ]] minted during the reign of [[ Archelaus I of Macedon ]] (r. 413–399 BC)

Brasidas died in 422 BC, the year Athens and Sparta struck an accord, the
[[ Peace of Nicias ]], that freed Macedonia from its obligations as an
Athenian ally.[[ ^40 ]] Following the 418 BC
[[ Battle of Mantinea | battle-of-mantinea-418-bc ]], the victorious Spartans
formed an alliance with [[ Argos | history-of-argos ]], a military pact
Perdiccas II was keen to join given the threat of Spartan allies remaining in
Chalcidice.[[ ^41 ]] When Argos suddenly switched sides as a pro-Athenian
[[ democracy ]], the Athenian navy was able to form a [[ blockade ]] against
Macedonian [[ seaports | seaport ]] and invade Chalcidice in 417 BC.[[ ^42 ]]
Perdiccas II sued for peace in 414 BC, forming an alliance with Athens that
was continued by his son and successor
[[ Archelaus I | archelaus-i-of-macedon ]] (r. 413–399 BC).[[ ^43 ]] Athens
then provided naval support to Archelaus I in the 410 BC Macedonian siege of
[[ Pydna ]], in exchange for timber and naval equipment.[[ ^44 ]]

Although Archelaus I was faced with some internal revolts and had to fend off
an invasion of Illyrians led by [[ Sirras ]] of Lynkestis, he was able to
project Macedonian power into Thessaly where he sent military aid to his
allies.[[ ^45 ]] Although he retained Aigai as a ceremonial and religious
centre, Archelaus I moved the [[ capital | capital-city ]] of the kingdom
north to [[ Pella ]], which was then positioned by a lake with a river
connecting it to the [[ Aegean Sea ]].[[ ^46 ]] He improved Macedonia's
[[ currency ]] by minting [[ coins | coin ]] with a
[[ higher silver content | silver-coin ]] as well as issuing separate
[[ copper coinage | coinage-metals ]].[[ ^47 ]] His royal court attracted the
presence of well-known intellectuals such as the Athenian [[ playwright ]]
[[ Euripides ]].[[ ^48 ]] When Archelaus I was assassinated (perhaps following
a [[ homosexual | homosexuality-in-ancient-greece ]] love affair with
[[ royal pages | royal-page ]] at his court), the kingdom was plunged into
chaos, in an era lasting from 399 to 393 BC that included the reign of four
different monarchs: [[ Orestes | orestes-of-macedon ]], son of Archelaus I;
[[ Aeropus II | aeropus-ii-of-macedon ]], uncle, [[ regent ]], and murderer of
Orestes; [[ Pausanias | pausanias-of-macedon ]], son of Aeropus II; and
[[ Amyntas II | amyntas-ii-of-macedon ]], who was married to the youngest
daughter of Archelaus I.[[ ^49 ]] Very little is known about this turbulent
period; it came to an end when [[ Amyntas III | amyntas-iii-of-macedon ]]
(r. 393–370 BC), son of Arrhidaeus and grandson of Amyntas I, killed Pausanias
and claimed the Macedonian throne.[[ ^50 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg/250px-Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg" height="126" width="250"

  <figcaption>A silver //[[ stater ]]// of [[ Amyntas III of Macedon ]] (r. 393–370 BC)

Amyntas III was forced to flee his kingdom in either 393 or 383 BC (based on
conflicting accounts), owing to a massive invasion by the [[ Illyrians ]] led
by [[ Bardylis ]].[[ ^4 ]] The [[ pretender ]] to the throne
[[ Argaeus | argaeus-ii-of-macedon ]] ruled in his absence, yet Amyntas III
eventually returned to his kingdom with the aid of Thessalian allies.[[ ^51 ]]
Amyntas III was also nearly overthrown by the forces of the Chalcidian city of
[[ Olynthos ]], but with the aid of [[ Teleutias ]], brother of the Spartan
king [[ Agesilaus II ]], the Macedonians forced Olynthos to surrender and
dissolve their [[ Chalcidian League ]] in 379 BC.[[ ^52 ]]

[[ Alexander II | alexander-ii-of-macedon ]] (r. 370–368 BC), son of
[[ Eurydice I | eurydice-i-of-macedon ]] and Amyntas III, succeeded his father
and immediately invaded Thessaly to wage war against the
//[[ tagus | tagus-title ]]// (supreme Thessalian military leader)
[[ Alexander of Pherae ]], capturing the city of [[ Larissa ]].[[ ^53 ]] The
Thessalians, desiring to remove both Alexander II and Alexander of Pherae as
their [[ overlords | overlord ]], appealed to [[ Pelopidas ]] of
[[ Thebes | thebes-greece ]] for aid; he succeeded in recapturing Larissa and,
in the peace agreement arranged with Macedonia, received aristocratic
[[ hostages | hostage ]] including Alexander II's brother and future king
[[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] (r. 359–336 BC).[[ ^54 ]] When
Alexander was assassinated by his brother-in-law [[ Ptolemy of Aloros ]], the
latter acted as an overbearing regent for
[[ Perdiccas III | perdiccas-iii-of-macedon ]] (r. 368–359 BC), younger
brother of Alexander II, who eventually had Ptolemy executed when reaching the
[[ age of majority ]] in 365 BC.[[ ^55 ]] The remainder of Perdiccas III's
reign was marked by political stability and financial recovery.[[ ^56 ]]
However, an Athenian invasion led by [[ Timotheus | timotheus-general ]], son
of [[ Conon ]], captured [[ Methone | methoni-pieria ]] and Pydna, and an
Illyrian invasion led by Bardylis succeeded in killing Perdiccas III and 4,000
Macedonian troops in battle.[[ ^57 ]]

=== Rise of Macedon

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Filip_II_Macedonia.jpg/250px-Filip_II_Macedonia.jpg" height="267" width="185"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg/250px-Philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" height="266" width="200"

Left, a bust of [[ Philip II of Macedon ]] (r.359–336BC) from the
[[ Hellenistic period ]], located at [[ Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek ]]. Right,
another bust of PhilipII, a 1st-century AD
[[ Roman copy | hellenistic-art#later-roman-copies ]] of a
[[ Hellenistic Greek | hellenistic-art ]] original, now in the
[[ Vatican Museums ]].

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Expansion_of_Macedon_%28English%29.svg/330px-Expansion_of_Macedon_%28English%29.svg.png" height="240" width="300"

  <figcaption>Map of the Kingdom of Macedon at the death of [[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] in 336 BC (light blue), with the original territory that existed in 431 BC (red outline), and [[ dependent states | dependent-state ]] (yellow)

Philip II was twenty-four years old when he acceded to the throne in
359 BC.[[ ^58 ]] Through the use of deft diplomacy, he was able to convince
the Thracians under [[ Berisades ]] to cease their support of
[[ Pausanias | pausanias-pretender ]], a pretender to the throne, and the
Athenians to halt their support of
[[ another pretender | argaeus-ii-of-macedon ]].[[ ^59 ]] He achieved these by
bribing the Thracians and their [[ Paeonian | paeonia-kingdom ]] allies and
establishing a treaty with Athens that relinquished his claims to
Amphipolis.[[ ^60 ]] He was also able to make peace with the Illyrians who
[[ had threatened his borders | battle-of-erigon-valley ]].[[ ^61 ]]

Philip II spent his initial years radically transforming the
[[ Macedonian army | ancient-macedonian-army ]]. A reform of its organization,
equipment, and training, including the introduction of the
[[ Macedonian phalanx ]] armed with [[ long pikes | pike-weapon ]] (i.e. the
//[[ sarissa ]]//), proved immediately successful when tested against his
Illyrian and Paeonian enemies.[[ ^62 ]] Confusing accounts in ancient sources
have led modern scholars to debate how much Philip II's royal predecessors may
have contributed to these reforms and the extent to which his ideas were
influenced by his [[ adolescent | adolescence ]] years of captivity in Thebes
as a political hostage during the [[ Theban hegemony ]], especially after
meeting with the general [[ Epaminondas ]].[[ ^63 ]]

The Macedonians, like the other Greeks, traditionally practised
[[ monogamy ]], but Philip II practised [[ polygamy ]] and married seven wives
with [[ perhaps only one | cleopatra-eurydice ]] that did not involve the
loyalty of his aristocratic subjects or new allies.[[ ^5 ]] His first
marriages were to [[ Phila of Elimeia ]] of the Upper Macedonian aristocracy
as well as the Illyrian princess [[ Audata ]] to ensure a marriage
alliance.[[ ^64 ]] To establish an alliance with Larissa in Thessaly, he
married the Thessalian noblewoman [[ Philinna ]] in 358 BC, who bore him a son
who would later rule as [[ Philip III Arrhidaeus ]] (r. 323–317 BC).[[ ^65 ]]
In 357 BC, he married [[ Olympias ]] to secure an alliance with
[[ Arybbas | arybbas-of-epirus ]], the [[ king of Epirus ]] and the
[[ Molossians ]]. This marriage would bear a son who would later rule as
Alexander III (better known as [[ Alexander the Great ]]) and claim descent
from the legendary [[ Achilles ]] by way of his
[[ dynastic heritage from Epirus | aeacidae ]].[[ ^66 ]] It is unclear whether
or not the Achaemenid Persian kings influenced Philip II's practice of
polygamy, although his predecessor Amyntas III had three sons with a possible
second wife Gygaea: Archelaus, Arrhidaeus, and
[[ Menelaus | menelaus-son-of-amyntas-iii ]].[[ ^67 ]] Philip II had Archelaus
put to death in 359 BC, while Philip II's other two half brothers fled to
Olynthos, serving as a //[[ casus belli ]]// for the [[ Olynthian War ]]
(349–348 BC) against the Chalcidian League.[[ ^68 ]]

While Athens was preoccupied with the [[ Social War (357–355 BC) ]], Philip II
retook Amphipolis from them in 357 BC and the following year recaptured Pydna
and Potidaea, the latter of which he handed over to the Chalcidian League as
promised in a treaty.[[ ^69 ]] In 356 BC, he took
[[ Crenides | crenides-macedonia ]], refounding it as [[ Philippi ]], while
his general [[ Parmenion ]] defeated the Illyrian king [[ Grabos II ]] of the
[[ Grabaei ]].[[ ^70 ]] During the 355–354 BC siege of Methone, Philip II lost
his right eye to an arrow wound, but managed to capture the city and treated
the inhabitants cordially, unlike the Potidaeans, who had been
enslaved.[[ ^6 ]]

Philip II then involved Macedonia in the [[ Third Sacred War ]] (356–346 BC).
It began when [[ Phocis | phocis-ancient-region ]] captured and plundered the
temple of [[ Apollo ]] at [[ Delphi ]] instead of submitting unpaid fines,
causing the [[ Amphictyonic League ]] to declare war on Phocis and a
[[ civil war ]] among the members of the [[ Thessalian League ]] aligned with
either Phocis or Thebes.[[ ^71 ]] Philip II's initial campaign against
[[ Pherae ]] in Thessaly in 353 BC at the behest of Larissa ended in two
disastrous defeats by the Phocian general [[ Onomarchus ]].[[ ^7 ]] Philip II
in turn defeated Onomarchus in 352 BC at the [[ Battle of Crocus Field ]],
which led to Philip II's election as leader (//[[ archon ]]//) of the
Thessalian League, provided him a seat on the Amphictyonic Council, and
allowed for a marriage alliance with Pherae by wedding [[ Nicesipolis ]],
niece of the tyrant [[ Jason of Pherae ]].[[ ^72 ]]

Philip II had some early involvement with the Achaemenid Empire, especially by
supporting [[ satraps ]] and mercenaries who rebelled against the central
authority of the Achaemenid king. The satrap of [[ Hellespontine Phrygia ]]
[[ Artabazos II ]], who was in rebellion against [[ Artaxerxes III ]], was
able to take refuge as an exile at the Macedonian court from 352 to 342 BC. He
was accompanied in exile by his family and by his mercenary general
[[ Memnon of Rhodes ]].[[ ^73 ]][[ ^74 ]] [[ Barsine ]], daughter of
Artabazos, and future wife of Alexander the Great, grew up at the Macedonian
court.[[ ^74 ]]

After campaigning against the Thracian ruler [[ Cersobleptes ]], in 349 BC,
Philip II began his war against the Chalcidian League, which had been
reestablished in 375 BC following a temporary disbandment.[[ ^75 ]] Despite an
Athenian intervention by [[ Charidemus ]],[[ ^76 ]] Olynthos was captured by
Philip II in 348 BC, and its inhabitants were
[[ sold into slavery | slavery-in-ancient-greece ]], including some
[[ Athenian citizens | athenian-citizenship ]].[[ ^77 ]] The Athenians,
especially in a series of speeches by [[ Demosthenes ]] known as the
//[[ Olynthiacs ]]//, were unsuccessful in persuading their allies to
counterattack and in 346 BC concluded a treaty with Macedonia
[[ known as the Peace | peace-of-philocrates ]] of [[ Philocrates ]].[[ ^78 ]]
The treaty stipulated that Athens would relinquish claims to Macedonian
coastal territories, the Chalcidice, and Amphipolis in return for the release
of the enslaved Athenians as well as guarantees that Philip II would not
attack Athenian settlements in the [[ Thracian Chersonese ]].[[ ^79 ]]
Meanwhile, Phocis and [[ Thermopylae ]] were captured by Macedonian forces,
the [[ Delphic temple | pythia ]] robbers were executed, and Philip II was
awarded the two Phocian seats on the Amphictyonic Council and the position of
[[ master of ceremonies ]] over the [[ Pythian Games ]].[[ ^80 ]] Athens
initially opposed his membership on the council and refused to attend the
games in protest, but they eventually accepted these conditions, perhaps after
some persuasion by Demosthenes in his oration //[[ On the Peace ]]//.[[ ^81 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Philip_II_of_Macedon_CdM.jpg/250px-Philip_II_of_Macedon_CdM.jpg" height="189" width="200"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Olympia_%2812%29.jpg/330px-Olympia_%2812%29.jpg" height="189" width="252"

Left, a //Niketerion// (victory medallion) bearing the effigy of king
[[ Philip II of Macedon ]], 3rd centuryAD, probably minted during the reign of
[[ Roman Emperor ]][[ Alexander Severus ]]. Right, the ruins of the
[[ Philippeion ]] at [[ Olympia, Greece ]], which was built by
[[ Philip II of Macedon ]] to celebrate his victory at the
[[ Battle of Chaeronea | battle-of-chaeronea-338-bc ]] in 338BC.[[ ^82 ]]

Over the next few years, Philip II reformed local governments in Thessaly,
campaigned against the Illyrian ruler [[ Pleuratus I ]], deposed Arybbas in
[[ Epirus | epirus-ancient-state ]] in favour of his brother-in-law
[[ Alexander I | alexander-i-of-epirus ]] (through Philip II's marriage to
Olympias), and defeated Cersebleptes in Thrace. This allowed him to extend
Macedonian control over the [[ Hellespont ]] in anticipation of an invasion
into [[ Achaemenid Anatolia | classical-anatolia ]].[[ ^83 ]] In 342 BC,
Philip II conquered [[ a Thracian city | history-of-plovdiv ]] in what is now
[[ Bulgaria ]] and renamed it [[ Philippopolis | philippopolis-thracia ]]
(modern [[ Plovdiv ]]).[[ ^84 ]] War broke out with Athens in 340 BC while
Philip II was engaged in two ultimately unsuccessful sieges of [[ Perinthus ]]
and [[ Byzantion ]], followed by a successful campaign against the Scythians
along the [[ Danube ]] and Macedonia's involvement in the
[[ Fourth Sacred War ]] against [[ Amphissa | amphissa-city ]] in
339 BC.[[ ^85 ]] Thebes ejected a Macedonian garrison from
[[ Nicaea (near Thermopylae) | nicaea-locris ]], leading Thebes to join
Athens, [[ Megara ]], Corinth, [[ Achaea ]], and [[ Euboea ]] in a final
confrontation against Macedonia at the
[[ Battle of Chaeronea | battle-of-chaeronea-338-bc ]] in 338 BC.[[ ^86 ]]
After the Macedonian victory at Chaeronea, Philip II installed an
[[ oligarchy ]] in Thebes, yet was lenient toward Athens, wishing to utilize
their navy in a planned invasion of the Achaemenid Empire.[[ ^87 ]] He was
then chiefly responsible for the formation of the [[ League of Corinth ]] that
included the major Greek city-states except Sparta. Despite the Kingdom of
Macedonia's official exclusion from the league, in 337 BC, Philip II was
elected as the leader (//[[ hegemon ]]//) of its council (//[[ synedrion ]]//)
and the [[ commander-in-chief ]] (//[[ strategos ]] [[ autokrator ]]//) of a
forthcoming campaign to invade the Achaemenid Empire.[[ ^88 ]] Philip's plan
to punish the Persians for the suffering of the Greeks and to liberate the
Greek cities of Asia Minor[[ ^89 ]] as well as perhaps the panhellenic fear of
another Persian invasion of Greece, contributed to his decision to invade the
Achaemenid Empire.[[ ^90 ]] The Persians offered aid to Perinthus and
Byzantion in 341–340 BC, highlighting Macedonia's strategic need to secure
Thrace and the Aegean Sea against increasing Achaemenid encroachment, as the
Persian king [[ Artaxerxes III ]] further consolidated his control over
satrapies in [[ western Anatolia | geography-of-anatolia ]].[[ ^91 ]] The
latter region, yielding far more wealth and valuable resources than the
Balkans, was also coveted by the Macedonian king for its sheer economic
potential.[[ ^92 ]]

When Philip II married
[[ Cleopatra Eurydice | cleopatra-eurydice-of-macedon ]], niece of general
[[ Attalus | attalus-general ]], talk of providing new potential heirs at the
wedding feast infuriated Philip II's son Alexander, a veteran of the Battle of
Chaeronea, and his mother Olympias.[[ ^93 ]] They fled together to Epirus
before Alexander was recalled to Pella by Philip II.[[ ^93 ]] When Philip II
arranged a marriage between his son Arrhidaeus and [[ Ada of Caria ]],
daughter of [[ Pixodarus ]], the Persian satrap of [[ Caria ]], Alexander
intervened and proposed to marry Ada instead. Philip II then cancelled the
wedding altogether and exiled Alexander's advisors [[ Ptolemy | ptolemy-i ]],
[[ Nearchus ]], and [[ Harpalus ]].[[ ^94 ]] To reconcile with Olympias,
Philip II had their daughter [[ Cleopatra | cleopatra-of-macedon ]] marry
Olympias' brother (and Cleopatra's uncle) Alexander I of Epirus, but Philip II
was assassinated by his bodyguard, [[ Pausanias of Orestis ]], during their
wedding feast and succeeded by Alexander in 336 BC.[[ ^95 ]]

=== Alexander's Empire

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/ACMA_1331_Alexander_2.JPG/250px-ACMA_1331_Alexander_2.JPG" height="200" width="160"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/AlexandreLouvre.jpg/250px-AlexandreLouvre.jpg" height="200" width="150"

Left, Bust of [[ Alexander the Great ]] by the Athenian sculptor
[[ Leochares ]], 330BC, [[ Acropolis Museum ]], Athens. Right, Bust of
Alexander the Great, a [[ Roman copy | roman-sculpture ]] of the
[[ Imperial Era | roman-empire ]] (1st or 2nd centuryAD) after an original
[[ bronze sculpture ]] made by the
[[ Greek sculptor | ancient-greek-sculpture ]][[ Lysippos ]], [[ Louvre ]],
Paris.

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/MacedonEmpire.jpg/330px-MacedonEmpire.jpg" height="143" width="300"

  <figcaption>Alexander's empire and his route

Modern scholars have argued over the possible role of
[[ Alexander III "the Great" | alexander-iii-of-macedon ]] and his mother
Olympias in the assassination of Philip II, noting the latter's choice to
exclude Alexander from his planned invasion of Asia, choosing instead for him
to act as regent of Greece and deputy //hegemon// of the League of Corinth,
and the potential bearing of another male heir between Philip II and his new
wife, Cleopatra Eurydice.[[ ^8 ]] Alexander III (r. 336–323 BC) was
immediately proclaimed king by [[ an assembly | popular-assembly ]] of the
army and leading aristocrats, chief among them being [[ Antipater ]] and
Parmenion.[[ ^96 ]] By the end of his reign and military career in 323 BC,
Alexander would rule over an empire consisting of [[ mainland Greece ]],
[[ Asia Minor ]], the [[ Levant ]], [[ ancient Egypt ]], [[ Mesopotamia ]],
[[ Persia ]], and much of [[ Central | central-asia ]] and [[ South Asia ]]
(i.e. modern [[ Pakistan ]]).[[ ^97 ]] Among his first acts was the burial of
his father at Aigai.[[ ^98 ]] The members of the League of Corinth revolted at
the news of Philip II's death, but were soon quelled by military force
alongside persuasive diplomacy, electing Alexander as //hegemon// of the
league to carry out the planned invasion of Achaemenid Persia.[[ ^99 ]]

In 335 BC, Alexander
[[ fought against the Thracian tribe | alexanders-balkan-campaign ]] of the
[[ Triballi ]] at [[ Haemus Mons ]] and along the [[ Danube ]], forcing their
surrender on [[ Peuce Island ]].[[ ^100 ]] Shortly thereafter, the Illyrian
chieftain [[ Cleitus | cleitus-son-of-bardylis ]], son of [[ Bardylis ]],
threatened to attack Macedonia with the aid of
[[ Glaucias | glaucias-of-taulantii ]], king of the [[ Taulantii ]], but
Alexander took the initiative and
[[ besieged the Illyrians | siege-of-pelium ]] at
[[ Pelion | pelion-illyria ]] (in modern [[ Albania ]]).[[ ^101 ]] When Thebes
had once again revolted from the League of Corinth and was besieging the
Macedonian garrison in the [[ Cadmea ]], Alexander left the Illyrian front and
marched to Thebes, which he
[[ placed under siege | battle-of-thebes ]].[[ ^102 ]] After breaching the
walls, Alexander's forces killed 6,000 Thebans, took 30,000 inhabitants as
[[ prisoners of war ]], and burned the city to the ground as a warning that
convinced all other Greek states except Sparta not to challenge Alexander
again.[[ ^103 ]]

Throughout his military career, Alexander won every battle that he personally
commanded.[[ ^104 ]] His first victory against the Persians in Asia Minor at
the [[ Battle of the Granicus ]] in 334 BC used a small cavalry contingent as
a distraction to allow his infantry to cross the river followed by a
[[ cavalry charge ]] from his [[ companion cavalry ]].[[ ^105 ]] Alexander led
the cavalry charge at the [[ Battle of Issus ]] in 333 BC, forcing the Persian
king [[ Darius III ]] and his army to flee.[[ ^105 ]] Darius III, despite
having superior numbers, was again forced to flee the
[[ Battle of Gaugamela ]] in 331 BC.[[ ^105 ]] The Persian king was later
captured and executed by his own satrap of [[ Bactria ]] and kinsman,
[[ Bessus ]], in 330 BC. The Macedonian king subsequently hunted down and
executed Bessus in what is now [[ Afghanistan ]], securing the region of
[[ Sogdia ]] in the process.[[ ^106 ]] At the 326 BC
[[ Battle of the Hydaspes ]] (modern-day [[ Punjab ]]), when the
[[ war elephants | war-elephant ]] of [[ King Porus ]] of the [[ Pauravas ]]
threatened Alexander's troops, he had them form open ranks to surround the
elephants and dislodge their handlers by using their //sarissa//
pikes.[[ ^107 ]] When his Macedonian troops threatened [[ mutiny ]] in 324 BC
at [[ Opis ]], [[ Babylonia ]] (near modern [[ Baghdad ]], [[ Iraq ]]),
Alexander offered Macedonian military titles and greater responsibilities to
Persian officers and units instead, forcing his troops to seek forgiveness at
a staged banquet of reconciliation between Persians and Macedonians.[[ ^108 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Stag_hunt_mosaic%2C_Pella.jpg/250px-Stag_hunt_mosaic%2C_Pella.jpg" height="157" width="250"

  <figcaption>The [[ Stag Hunt Mosaic ]], c. 300 BC, from [[ Pella ]]; the figure on the right is possibly [[ Alexander the Great ]] due to the date of the [[ mosaic ]] along with the depicted upsweep of his centrally-parted hair (//anastole//); the figure on the left wielding a double-edged axe (associated with [[ Hephaistos ]]) is perhaps [[ Hephaestion ]], one of Alexander's loyal companions.

Alexander perhaps undercut his own rule by demonstrating signs of
[[ megalomania | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/megalomania ]].[[ ^109 ]]
While utilizing effective propaganda such as the cutting of the
[[ Gordian Knot ]], he also attempted to portray himself as a
[[ living god | sacred-king ]] and son of Zeus following his visit to the
[[ oracle ]] at [[ Siwah ]] in the [[ Libyan Desert ]] (in modern-day Egypt)
in 331 BC.[[ ^110 ]] His attempt in 327 BC to have his men prostrate before
him in [[ Bactra ]] in an act of //[[ proskynesis ]]// borrowed from the
Persian kings was rejected as religious blasphemy by his Macedonian and Greek
subjects after his court historian [[ Callisthenes ]] refused to perform this
ritual.[[ ^109 ]] When Alexander had Parmenion murdered at [[ Ecbatana ]]
(near modern [[ Hamadan ]], [[ Iran ]]) in 330 BC, this was "symptomatic of
the growing gulf between the king's interests and those of his country and
people", according to Errington.[[ ^111 ]] His murder of
[[ Cleitus the Black ]] in 328 BC is described as "vengeful and reckless" by
Dawn L. Gilley and Ian Worthington.[[ ^112 ]] Continuing the polygamous habits
of his father, Alexander encouraged his men to marry native women in Asia,
leading by example when he wed [[ Roxana ]], a Sogdian princess of
Bactria.[[ ^113 ]] He then married [[ Stateira II ]], eldest daughter of
Darius III, and [[ Parysatis II ]], youngest daughter of [[ Artaxerxes III ]],
at the [[ Susa weddings ]] in 324 BC.[[ ^114 ]]

Meanwhile, in Greece, the [[ Spartan king ]] [[ Agis III ]] attempted to lead
a rebellion of the Greeks against Macedonia.[[ ^115 ]] He was defeated in
331 BC at the [[ Battle of Megalopolis ]] by Antipater, who was serving as
regent of Macedonia and deputy //hegemon// of the League of Corinth in
Alexander's stead.[[ ^9 ]] Before Antipater embarked on his campaign in the
[[ Peloponnese ]], Memnon, the governor of Thrace, was dissuaded from
rebellion by use of diplomacy.[[ ^116 ]] Antipater deferred the punishment of
Sparta to the League of Corinth headed by Alexander, who ultimately pardoned
the Spartans on the condition that they submit fifty nobles as
hostages.[[ ^117 ]] Antipater's hegemony was somewhat unpopular in Greece due
to his practice (perhaps by order of Alexander) of exiling malcontents and
garrisoning cities with Macedonian troops, yet in 330 BC, Alexander declared
that the [[ tyrannies ]] installed in Greece were to be abolished and Greek
freedom was to be restored.[[ ^118 ]]

=== Diadochi

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Diadochi_LA.svg/330px-Diadochi_LA.svg.png" height="144" width="300"

  <figcaption>Kingdoms of the [[ Diadochi ]] c. 301 BC, after the [[ Battle of Ipsus ]]
  Kingdom of [[ Ptolemy I Soter ]]
  Kingdom of [[ Cassander ]]
  Kingdom of [[ Lysimachus ]]
  Kingdom of [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]]
  [[ Epirus ]]
Other
  [[ Carthage ]]
  [[ Roman Republic ]]
  [[ Greek States | ancient-greece ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg/250px-Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg" height="121" width="250"

  <figcaption>A golden //[[ stater ]]// of [[ Philip III Arrhidaeus ]] (r. 323–317 BC) bearing images of [[ Athena ]] (left) and [[ Nike | nike-mythology ]] (right)

When [[ Alexander the Great died | death-of-alexander-the-great ]] at
[[ Babylon ]] in 323 BC, his mother Olympias immediately accused Antipater and
his faction of poisoning him, although there is no evidence to confirm
this.[[ ^119 ]] With no official [[ heir apparent ]], the Macedonian military
command split, with one side proclaiming Alexander's half-brother Philip III
Arrhidaeus (r. 323–317 BC) as king and the other siding with the infant son of
Alexander and Roxana, [[ Alexander IV | alexander-iv-of-macedon ]]
(r. 323–309 BC).[[ ^120 ]] Except for the Euboeans and Boeotians, the Greeks
also immediately rose up in a rebellion against Antipater known as the
[[ Lamian War ]] (323–322 BC).[[ ^121 ]] When Antipater was defeated at the
323 BC [[ Battle of Thermopylae | battle-of-thermopylae-323-bc ]], he fled to
[[ Lamia | lamia-city ]] where he was besieged by the Athenian commander
[[ Leosthenes ]]. A Macedonian army led by [[ Leonnatus ]] rescued Antipater
by lifting the siege.[[ ^122 ]] Antipater defeated the rebellion, yet his
death in 319 BC left a power vacuum wherein the two proclaimed kings of
Macedonia became pawns in [[ a power struggle | wars-of-the-diadochi ]]
between the //[[ diadochi ]]//, the former generals of Alexander's
army.[[ ^123 ]]

A [[ council of the army convened in Babylon | partition-of-babylon ]]
immediately after Alexander's death, naming Philip III as king and the
[[ chiliarch ]] [[ Perdiccas ]] as his regent.[[ ^124 ]] Antipater,
[[ Antigonus Monophthalmus ]], [[ Craterus ]], and Ptolemy formed a coalition
against Perdiccas in a civil war initiated by Ptolemy's
[[ seizure of the hearse of Alexander the Great | tomb-of-alexander-the-great ]].[[ ^125 ]]
Perdiccas was assassinated in 321 BC by his own officers during a failed
campaign in Egypt against Ptolemy, where his march along the [[ Nile River ]]
resulted in the drowning of 2,000 of his men.[[ ^126 ]] Although
[[ Eumenes of Cardia ]] managed to kill Craterus in battle, this had little to
no effect on the outcome of the 321 BC [[ Partition of Triparadisus ]] in
[[ Syria | syria-region ]] where the victorious coalition settled the issue of
a new regency and territorial rights.[[ ^127 ]] Antipater was appointed as
regent over the two kings. Before Antipater died in 319 BC, he named the
staunch Argead loyalist [[ Polyperchon ]] as his successor, passing over his
own son [[ Cassander ]] and ignoring the right of the king to choose a new
regent (since Philip III was considered mentally unstable), in effect
bypassing the council of the army as well.[[ ^128 ]]

Forming an alliance with Ptolemy, Antigonus, and [[ Lysimachus ]], Cassander
had his officer [[ Nicanor | nicanor-antipatrid-general ]] capture the
[[ Munichia ]] fortress of Athens' port town [[ Piraeus ]] in defiance of
Polyperchon's decree that Greek cities should be free of Macedonian garrisons,
sparking the [[ Second War of the Diadochi ]] (319–315 BC).[[ ^129 ]] Given a
string of military failures by Polyperchon, in 317 BC, Philip III, by way of
his politically engaged wife [[ Eurydice II of Macedon ]], officially replaced
him as regent with Cassander.[[ ^130 ]] Afterwards, Polyperchon desperately
sought the aid of Olympias in Epirus.[[ ^130 ]] A joint force of Epirotes,
Aetolians, and Polyperchon's troops invaded Macedonia and forced the surrender
of Philip III and Eurydice's army, allowing Olympias to execute the king and
force his queen to commit suicide.[[ ^131 ]] Olympias then had Nicanor and
dozens of other Macedonian nobles killed, but by the spring of 316 BC,
Cassander had defeated her forces, captured her, and placed her on trial for
murder before sentencing her to death.[[ ^132 ]]

Cassander married Philip II's daughter
[[ Thessalonike | thessalonike-of-macedon ]] and briefly extended Macedonian
control into Illyria as far as [[ Epidamnos ]] (modern [[ Durrës ]], Albania).
By 313 BC, it was retaken by the Illyrian king
[[ Glaucias of Taulantii ]].[[ ^133 ]] By 316 BC, Antigonus had taken the
territory of Eumenes and managed to eject [[ Seleucus Nicator ]] from his
Babylonian satrapy, leading Cassander, Ptolemy, and Lysimachus to issue a
joint ultimatum to Antigonus in 315 BC for him to surrender various
territories in Asia.[[ ^11 ]] Antigonus promptly allied with Polyperchon, now
based in Corinth, and issued an ultimatum of his own to Cassander, charging
him with murder for executing Olympias and demanding that he hand over the
royal family, King Alexander IV and the [[ queen mother ]] Roxana.[[ ^134 ]]
The conflict that followed lasted until the winter of 312/311 BC, when a new
peace settlement recognized Cassander as general of Europe, Antigonus as
"first in Asia", Ptolemy as general of Egypt, and Lysimachus as general of
Thrace.[[ ^135 ]] Cassander had Alexander IV and Roxana put to death in the
winter of 311/310 BC, and between 306 and 305 BC the //diadochi// were
declared kings of their respective territories.[[ ^136 ]]

=== Hellenistic era

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg/250px-Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg" height="189" width="142"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Demetrios_Poliorketes_MAN_Napoli_Inv6149.jpg/250px-Demetrios_Poliorketes_MAN_Napoli_Inv6149.jpg" height="189" width="142"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Seleuco_I_2.JPG/250px-Seleuco_I_2.JPG" height="230" width="161"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Lisimaco_%28c.d.%29%2C_copia_augustea_%2823_ac-14_dc%29_da_orig._del_II_sec_ac._6141.JPG/250px-Lisimaco_%28c.d.%29%2C_copia_augustea_%2823_ac-14_dc%29_da_orig._del_II_sec_ac._6141.JPG" height="230" width="123"

The portrait busts of [[ Pyrrhus of Epirus ]] (top left),
[[ Demetrius I of Macedon ]] (top right), [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]] (bottom
left), and [[ Lysimachus ]] (bottom right),
[[ Roman-era copies | roman-portraiture ]] of
[[ Hellenistic Greek originals | hellenistic-sculpture ]] from the
[[ Villa of the Papyri ]] at [[ Herculaneum ]],
[[ Naples National Archaeological Museum ]]

The beginning of [[ Hellenistic Greece ]] was defined by the struggle between
the [[ Antipatrid dynasty ]], led first by [[ Cassander ]] (r. 305–297 BC),
son of Antipater, and the [[ Antigonid dynasty ]], led by the Macedonian
general [[ Antigonus I Monophthalmus ]] (r. 306–301 BC) and his son, the
future king [[ Demetrius I | demetrius-i-of-macedon ]] (r. 294–288 BC).
Cassander besieged Athens in 303 BC, but was forced to retreat to Macedonia
when Demetrius invaded [[ Boeotia ]] to his rear, attempting to sever his path
of retreat.[[ ^137 ]] While Antigonus and Demetrius attempted to recreate
Philip II's [[ Hellenic league | league-of-corinth ]] with themselves as dual
hegemons, a revived coalition of Cassander, [[ Ptolemy I Soter ]]
(r. 305–283 BC) of Egypt's [[ Ptolemaic dynasty ]], [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]]
(r. 305–281 BC) of the [[ Seleucid Empire ]], and Lysimachus (r. 306–281 BC),
[[ King of Thrace | list-of-rulers-of-thrace-and-dacia ]], defeated the
Antigonids at the [[ Battle of Ipsus ]] in 301 BC, killing Antigonus and
forcing Demetrius into flight.[[ ^138 ]]

Cassander died in 297 BC, and his sickly son
[[ Philip IV | philip-iv-of-macedon ]] died the same year, succeeded by
Cassander's other sons [[ Alexander V of Macedon ]] (r. 297–294 BC) and
[[ Antipater II of Macedon ]] (r. 297–294 BC), with their mother
[[ Thessalonike of Macedon ]] acting as regent.[[ ^139 ]] While Demetrius
fought against the Antipatrid forces in Greece, Antipater II killed his own
mother to obtain power.[[ ^139 ]] His desperate brother Alexander V then
requested aid from [[ Pyrrhus of Epirus ]] (r. 297–272 BC),[[ ^139 ]] who had
fought alongside Demetrius at the Battle of Ipsus, but was sent to Egypt as a
hostage as part of an agreement between Demetrius and Ptolemy I.[[ ^140 ]] In
exchange for defeating the forces of Antipater II and forcing him to flee to
the court of Lysimachus in Thrace, Pyrrhus was awarded the westernmost
portions of the Macedonian kingdom.[[ ^141 ]] Demetrius had his nephew
Alexander V assassinated and was then proclaimed king of Macedonia, but his
subjects protested against his aloof, Eastern-style [[ autocracy ]].[[ ^139 ]]

War broke out between Pyrrhus and Demetrius in 290 BC when
[[ Lanassa, wife of Pyrrhus ]], daughter of [[ Agathocles of Syracuse ]], left
him for Demetrius and offered him her [[ dowry ]] of [[ Corcyra ]].[[ ^142 ]]
The war dragged on until 288 BC, when Demetrius lost the support of
[[ the Macedonians | ancient-macedonians ]] and fled the country. Macedonia
was then divided between Pyrrhus and Lysimachus, the former taking
[[ western Macedonia ]] and the latter eastern Macedonia.[[ ^142 ]] By 286 BC,
Lysimachus had expelled Pyrrhus and his forces from Macedonia.[[ ^10 ]] In
282 BC, a new war erupted between Seleucus I and Lysimachus; the latter was
killed in the [[ Battle of Corupedion ]], allowing Seleucus I to take control
of Thrace and Macedonia.[[ ^143 ]] In two dramatic reversals of fortune,
Seleucus I was assassinated in 281 BC by his officer [[ Ptolemy Keraunos ]],
son of Ptolemy I and grandson of Antipater, who was then proclaimed king of
Macedonia before being killed in battle in 279 BC by
[[ Celtic invaders | galatians-people ]] in the
[[ Gallic invasion of Greece ]].[[ ^144 ]] The Macedonian army proclaimed the
general [[ Sosthenes of Macedon ]] as king, although he apparently refused the
title.[[ ^145 ]] After defeating the [[ Gallic | gauls ]] ruler [[ Bolgios ]]
and driving out the raiding party of [[ Brennus | brennus-3rd-century-bc ]],
Sosthenes died and left a chaotic situation in Macedonia.[[ ^146 ]] The Gallic
invaders ravaged Macedonia until [[ Antigonus Gonatas ]], son of Demetrius,
defeated them in Thrace at the 277 BC [[ Battle of Lysimachia ]] and was then
proclaimed king [[ Antigonus II of Macedon ]]
(r. 277–274, 272–239 BC).[[ ^147 ]]

In 280 BC, Pyrrhus embarked on a campaign in [[ Magna Graecia ]] (i.e.
[[ southern Italy ]]) against the [[ Roman Republic ]] known as the
[[ Pyrrhic War ]], followed by his
[[ invasion of Sicily | siege-of-syracuse-278-bc ]].[[ ^148 ]] Ptolemy
Keraunos secured his position on the Macedonian throne by giving Pyrrhus five
thousand soldiers and twenty [[ war elephants | war-elephant ]] for this
endeavor.[[ ^140 ]] Pyrrhus returned to Epirus in 275 BC after the ultimate
failure of both campaigns, which contributed to the [[ rise of Rome ]] because
[[ Greek cities in southern Italy | colonies-in-antiquity ]] such as
[[ Tarentum | taranto ]] now became Roman allies.[[ ^148 ]] Pyrrhus invaded
Macedonia in 274 BC, defeating the largely mercenary army of Antigonus II at
the 274 BC [[ Battle of Aous | battle-of-the-aous-274-bc ]] and driving him
out of Macedonia, forcing him to seek refuge with his naval fleet in the
Aegean.[[ ^149 ]]

==== Antigonid Macedon

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_Tomb_of_Lyson_and_Kallikles_a72b9b998c2e98a1390dbae9e032ea1c.jpg/250px-Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_Tomb_of_Lyson_and_Kallikles_a72b9b998c2e98a1390dbae9e032ea1c.jpg" height="159" width="250"

  <figcaption>Paintings of [[ Hellenistic ]]-era military arms and armor from a tomb in ancient [[ Mieza | mieza-macedonia ]] (modern-day Lefkadia), [[ Imathia ]], [[ Central Macedonia ]], [[ Greece ]], 2nd century BC

Pyrrhus lost much of his support among the Macedonians in 273 BC when his
unruly Gallic mercenaries plundered the royal cemetery of Aigai.[[ ^150 ]]
Pyrrhus pursued Antigonus II in the Peloponnese, yet Antigonus II was
ultimately able to recapture Macedonia.[[ ^151 ]] Pyrrhus was killed while
besieging [[ Argos | argos-peloponnese ]] in 272 BC, allowing Antigonus II to
reclaim the rest of Greece.[[ ^152 ]] He then restored the Argead dynastic
graves at Aigai and annexed the
[[ Kingdom of Paeonia | paeonia-kingdom ]].[[ ^153 ]]

The [[ Aetolian League ]] hampered Antigonus II's control over
[[ central Greece | central-greece-geographic-region ]], and the formation of
the [[ Achaean League ]] in 251 BC pushed Macedonian forces out of much of the
Peloponnese and at times incorporated Athens and Sparta.[[ ^154 ]] While the
Seleucid Empire aligned with Antigonid Macedonia against Ptolemaic Egypt
during the [[ Syrian Wars ]], the Ptolemaic navy heavily disrupted
Antigonus II's efforts to control mainland Greece.[[ ^155 ]] With the aid of
the Ptolemaic navy, the Athenian statesman [[ Chremonides ]] led a revolt
against Macedonian authority known as the [[ Chremonidean War ]]
(267–261 BC).[[ ^156 ]] By 265 BC, Athens was surrounded and besieged by
Antigonus II's forces, and a Ptolemaic fleet was defeated in the
[[ Battle of Cos ]]. Athens finally surrendered in 261 BC.[[ ^157 ]] After
Macedonia formed an alliance with the Seleucid ruler [[ Antiochus II ]], a
peace settlement between Antigonus II and [[ Ptolemy II Philadelphus ]] of
Egypt was finally struck in 255 BC.[[ ^158 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Apollon_Tempel_im_antiken_Korinth.jpg/250px-Apollon_Tempel_im_antiken_Korinth.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>The [[ Temple | ancient-greek-temple ]] of [[ Apollo ]] at [[ Corinth | ancient-corinth ]], built c. 540 BC, with the [[ Acrocorinth ]] (i.e. the [[ acropolis ]] of Corinth that once held a [[ Macedonian garrison | antigonid-macedonian-army ]])[[ ^159 ]] seen in the background

In 251 BC, [[ Aratus of Sicyon ]] led a rebellion against Antigonus II, and in
250 BC, Ptolemy II declared his support for the self-proclaimed King
[[ Alexander of Corinth ]].[[ ^160 ]] Although Alexander died in 246 BC and
Antigonus was able to score a naval victory against the Ptolemies
[[ at Andros | battle-of-andros-246-bc ]], the Macedonians lost the
[[ Acrocorinth ]] to the forces of Aratus in 243 BC, followed by the induction
of Corinth into the Achaean League.[[ ^161 ]] Antigonus II made peace with the
Achaean League in 240 BC, ceding the territories that he had lost in
Greece.[[ ^162 ]] Antigonus II died in 239 BC and was succeeded by his son
[[ Demetrius II of Macedon ]] (r. 239–229 BC). Seeking an alliance with
Macedonia to defend against the Aetolians, the [[ queen mother ]] and regent
of Epirus, [[ Olympias II | olympias-ii-of-epirus ]], offered her daughter
[[ Phthia of Macedon ]] to Demetrius II in marriage. Demetrius II accepted her
proposal, but he damaged relations with the Seleucids by divorcing
[[ Stratonice of Macedon ]].[[ ^163 ]] Although the Aetolians formed an
alliance with the Achaean League as a result, Demetrius II was able to invade
Boeotia and capture it from the Aetolians by 236 BC.[[ ^159 ]]

The Achaean League managed to capture [[ Megalopolis | megalopolis-greece ]]
in 235 BC, and by the end of Demetrius II's reign most of the Peloponnese
except Argos was taken from the Macedonians.[[ ^164 ]] Demetrius II also lost
an ally [[ in Epirus | epirus-ancient-state ]] when the
[[ monarchy was toppled | deidamia-ii-of-epirus ]] in a
[[ republican revolution | epirote-league ]].[[ ^165 ]] Demetrius II enlisted
the aid of the [[ Illyrian | illyria ]] king [[ Agron | agron-of-illyria ]] to
defend [[ Acarnania ]] against Aetolia, and in 229 BC, they managed to defeat
the combined navies of the Aetolian and Achaean Leagues at the
[[ Battle of Paxos ]].[[ ^165 ]] Another Illyrian ruler, [[ Longarus ]] of the
[[ Dardanian Kingdom ]], invaded Macedonia and defeated an army of
Demetrius II shortly before his death in 229 BC.[[ ^166 ]] Although his young
son [[ Philip | philip-v-of-macedon ]] immediately inherited the throne, his
regent [[ Antigonus III Doson ]] (r. 229–221 BC), nephew of Antigonus II, was
proclaimed king by the army, with Philip as his heir, following a string of
military victories against the Illyrians in the north and the Aetolians in
Thessaly.[[ ^167 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Tetradrachm%2C_229-221%2C_Antigonos_Doson.jpg/250px-Tetradrachm%2C_229-221%2C_Antigonos_Doson.jpg" height="118" width="250"

  <figcaption>A //[[ tetradrachm ]]// minted during the reign of [[ Antigonus III Doson ]] (r. 229–221 BC), possibly at [[ Amphipolis ]], bearing the portrait image of [[ Poseidon ]] on the [[ obverse ]] and on the reverse a scene depicting [[ Apollo ]] sitting on the [[ prow of a ship | hellenistic-era-warships ]]

Aratus sent an embassy to Antigonus III in 226 BC seeking an unexpected
alliance now that the reformist king [[ Cleomenes III ]] of Sparta was
threatening the rest of Greece in the [[ Cleomenean War ]]
(229–222 BC).[[ ^168 ]] In exchange for military aid, Antigonus III demanded
the return of Corinth to Macedonian control, which Aratus finally agreed to in
225 BC.[[ ^169 ]] In 224 BC, Antigonus III's forces took
[[ Arcadia | arcadia-ancient-region ]] from Sparta. After forming a Hellenic
league in the same vein as Philip II's League of Corinth, he managed to defeat
Sparta at the [[ Battle of Sellasia ]] in 222 BC.[[ ^170 ]] Sparta was
occupied by a foreign power for the first time in its history, restoring
Macedonia's position as the leading power in Greece.[[ ^171 ]] Antigonus died
a year later, perhaps from [[ tuberculosis ]], leaving behind a strong
[[ Hellenistic kingdom | hellenistic-period ]] for his successor
Philip V.[[ ^172 ]]

[[ Philip V of Macedon ]] (r. 221–179 BC) faced immediate challenges to his
authority by the Illyrian [[ Dardani ]] and Aetolian League.[[ ^173 ]]
Philip V and his allies were successful against the Aetolians and their allies
in the [[ Social War (220–217 BC) ]], yet he made peace with the Aetolians
once he heard of incursions by the Dardani in the north and the
[[ Carthaginian | history-of-carthage ]] victory over
[[ the Romans | history-of-the-roman-republic ]] at the
[[ Battle of Lake Trasimene ]] in 217 BC.[[ ^174 ]] [[ Demetrius of Pharos ]]
is alleged to have convinced Philip V to first
[[ secure Illyria | illyrian-wars ]] in advance of an invasion of the
[[ Italian peninsula ]].[[ ^11 ]] In 216 BC, Philip V sent a hundred
[[ light warships | hellenistic-era-warships ]] into the [[ Adriatic Sea ]] to
attack Illyria, a move that prompted [[ Scerdilaidas ]] of the
[[ Ardiaean Kingdom ]] to appeal to the Romans for aid.[[ ^175 ]] Rome
responded by sending ten heavy [[ quinqueremes | quinquereme ]] from
[[ Roman Sicily ]] to patrol the Illyrian coasts, causing Philip V to reverse
course and order his fleet to retreat, averting open conflict for the time
being.[[ ^176 ]]

=== Conflict with Rome

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png/330px-Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png" height="240" width="300"

  <figcaption>The Kingdom of Macedonia (orange) under [[ Philip V | philip-v-of-macedon ]] (r. 221–179 BC), with Macedonian [[ dependent states | dependent-state ]] (dark yellow), the [[ Seleucid Empire ]] (bright yellow), [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] [[ protectorates | protectorate ]] (dark green), the [[ Kingdom of Pergamon ]] (light green), independent states (light purple), and possessions of the [[ Ptolemaic Empire ]] (violet purple)

In 215 BC, at the height of the [[ Second Punic War ]] with the
[[ Carthaginian Empire ]], [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] authorities intercepted
a ship off the [[ Calabrian | calabria ]] coast holding a Macedonian envoy and
a Carthaginian ambassador in possession of a treaty composed by [[ Hannibal ]]
declaring an alliance with Philip V.[[ ^177 ]]
[[ The treaty | macedoniancarthaginian-treaty ]] stipulated that
[[ Carthage | history-of-carthage ]] had the sole right to negotiate the terms
of Rome's hypothetical surrender and promised mutual aid if a resurgent Rome
should seek revenge against either Macedonia or Carthage.[[ ^178 ]] Although
the Macedonians were perhaps only interested in safeguarding their newly
conquered territories in Illyria,[[ ^179 ]] the Romans were nevertheless able
to thwart whatever grand ambitions Philip V had for the Adriatic region during
the [[ First Macedonian War ]] (214–205 BC). In 214 BC, Rome positioned a
[[ naval fleet | roman-navy ]] at [[ Oricus ]], which was assaulted along with
[[ Apollonia | apollonia-illyria ]] by Macedonian forces.[[ ^180 ]] When the
Macedonians captured [[ Lissus | lissus-illyria ]] in 212 BC, the
[[ Roman Senate ]] responded by inciting the Aetolian League, Sparta,
[[ Elis | ancient-elis ]], [[ Messenia ]], and [[ Attalus I ]] (r. 241–197 BC)
of [[ Pergamon ]] to wage war against Philip V, keeping him occupied and away
from Italy.[[ ^181 ]]

The Aetolian League concluded a [[ peace agreement ]] with Philip V in 206 BC,
and the [[ Roman Republic ]] negotiated the [[ Treaty of Phoenice ]] in
205 BC, ending the war and allowing the Macedonians to retain some captured
settlements in Illyria.[[ ^182 ]] Although the Romans rejected an Aetolian
request in 202 BC for Rome to declare war on Macedonia once again, the Roman
Senate gave serious consideration to the similar offer made by Pergamon and
its ally [[ Rhodes ]] in 201 BC.[[ ^183 ]] These states were concerned about
Philip V's alliance with [[ Antiochus III the Great ]] of the Seleucid Empire,
which invaded the war-weary and financially exhausted Ptolemaic Empire in the
[[ Fifth Syrian War ]] (202–195 BC) as Philip V captured Ptolemaic settlements
in the Aegean Sea.[[ ^184 ]] Although Rome's envoys played a critical role in
convincing Athens to join the anti-Macedonian alliance with Pergamon and
Rhodes in 200 BC, the //[[ comitia centuriata ]]// (people's assembly)
rejected the Roman Senate's proposal for a declaration of war on
Macedonia.[[ ^185 ]] Meanwhile, Philip V conquered territories in the
[[ Hellespont ]] and [[ Bosporus ]] as well as Ptolemaic [[ Samos ]], which
led Rhodes to [[ form an alliance with Pergamon | cretan-war-205200-bc ]],
[[ Byzantium ]], [[ Cyzicus ]], and [[ Chios ]] against Macedonia.[[ ^186 ]]
Despite Philip V's nominal alliance with the Seleucid king, he lost the naval
[[ Battle of Chios | battle-of-chios-201-bc ]] in 201 BC and was blockaded at
[[ Bargylia ]] by the Rhodian and Pergamene navies.[[ ^187 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Philip_V._221-179_BCE.jpg/250px-Philip_V._221-179_BCE.jpg" height="106" width="250"

  <figcaption>A [[ tetradrachm ]] of [[ Philip V of Macedon ]] (r. 221–179 BC), with the king's portrait on the [[ obverse ]] and [[ Athena Alkidemos ]] brandishing a thunderbolt on the reverse

While Philip V was busy fighting Rome's Greek allies, Rome viewed this as an
opportunity to punish this former ally of Hannibal with a war that they hoped
would supply a victory and require few resources.[[ ^12 ]] The Roman Senate
demanded that Philip V cease hostilities against neighbouring Greek powers and
defer to an international arbitration committee for settling
grievances.[[ ^188 ]] When the //comitia centuriata// finally voted in
approval of the Roman Senate's declaration of war in 200 BC and handed their
[[ ultimatum ]] to Philip V, demanding that a [[ tribunal ]] assess the
damages owed to Rhodes and Pergamon, the Macedonian king rejected it. This
marked the beginning of the [[ Second Macedonian War ]] (200–197 BC), with
[[ Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus ]] spearheading
[[ military operations | military-operation ]] in Apollonia.[[ ^189 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Eumene_II%2C_fondatore_della_biblioteca_di_pergamo%2C_copia_romana_%2850_dc_ca%29_da_orig%2C._ellenistico_su_busto_moderno%2C_MANN_02.JPG/250px-Eumene_II%2C_fondatore_della_biblioteca_di_pergamo%2C_copia_romana_%2850_dc_ca%29_da_orig%2C._ellenistico_su_busto_moderno%2C_MANN_02.JPG" height="326" width="190"

  <figcaption>Bronze bust of [[ Eumenes II ]] of [[ Pergamon ]], a [[ Roman copy | roman-sculpture ]] of a [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-art ]] [[ Greek original | ancient-greek-sculpture ]], from the [[ Villa of the Papyri ]] in [[ Herculaneum ]]

The Macedonians successfully defended their territory for roughly two
years,[[ ^190 ]] but the [[ Roman consul ]] [[ Titus Quinctius Flamininus ]]
managed to expel Philip V from Macedonia in 198 BC, forcing his men to take
refuge in Thessaly.[[ ^191 ]] When the Achaean League switched their loyalties
from Macedonia to Rome, the Macedonian king sued for peace, but the terms
offered were considered too stringent, and so the war continued.[[ ^191 ]] In
June 197 BC, the Macedonians were defeated at the
[[ Battle of Cynoscephalae ]].[[ ^192 ]] Rome then ratified a treaty that
forced Macedonia to relinquish control of much of its Greek possessions
outside of Macedonia proper, if only to act as a buffer against Illyrian and
Thracian incursions into Greece.[[ ^193 ]] Although some Greeks suspected
Roman intentions of supplanting Macedonia as the new hegemonic power in
Greece, Flaminius announced at the [[ Isthmian Games ]] of 196 BC that Rome
intended to preserve Greek [[ liberty ]] by leaving behind no garrisons and by
not exacting [[ tribute ]] of any kind.[[ ^194 ]] His promise was delayed by
negotiations with the Spartan king [[ Nabis | nabis-of-sparta ]], who had
meanwhile captured Argos, yet Roman forces evacuated Greece in
194 BC.[[ ^195 ]]

Encouraged by the Aetolian League and their calls to liberate Greece from the
Romans, the [[ Seleucid king | seleucid-dynasty ]] Antiochus III landed with
his army at [[ Demetrias ]], Thessaly, in 192 BC, and was elected
//strategos// by the Aetolians.[[ ^196 ]] Macedonia, the Achaean League, and
other Greek city-states maintained their alliance with Rome.[[ ^197 ]] The
Romans [[ defeated the Seleucids | romanseleucid-war ]] in the 191 BC
[[ Battle of Thermopylae | battle-of-thermopylae-191-bc ]] as well as the
[[ Battle of Magnesia ]] in 190 BC, forcing the Seleucids to pay a
[[ war indemnity ]], dismantle most of its navy, and abandon its claims to any
territories north or west of the [[ Taurus Mountains ]] in the 188 BC
[[ Treaty of Apamea ]].[[ ^198 ]] With Rome's acceptance, Philip V was able to
capture some cities in central Greece in 191–189 BC that had been allied to
Antiochus III, while Rhodes and [[ Eumenes II ]] (r. 197–159 BC) of Pergamon
gained territories in Asia Minor.[[ ^199 ]]

Failing to please all sides in various territorial disputes, the Roman Senate
decided in 184/183 BC to force Philip V to abandon [[ Aenus | aenus-thrace ]]
and [[ Maronea | maroneia ]], since these had been declared free cities in the
Treaty of Apamea.[[ ^13 ]] This assuaged the fear of Eumenes II that Macedonia
could pose a threat to his lands in the Hellespont.[[ ^200 ]]
[[ Perseus of Macedon ]] (r. 179–168 BC) succeeded Philip V and executed
[[ his brother Demetrius | demetrius-son-of-philip-v ]], who had been favoured
by the Romans but was charged by Perseus with [[ high treason ]].[[ ^201 ]]
Perseus then attempted to form marriage alliances with
[[ Prusias II of Bithynia ]] and [[ Seleucus IV Philopator ]] of the Seleucid
Empire, along with renewed relations with Rhodes that greatly unsettled
Eumenes II.[[ ^202 ]] Although Eumenes II attempted to undermine these
diplomatic relationships, Perseus fostered an alliance with the
[[ Boeotian League ]], extended his authority into Illyria
[[ and Thrace | abrupolis ]], and in 174 BC, won the role of managing the
Temple of Apollo at Delphi as a member of the
[[ Amphictyonic Council ]].[[ ^203 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Perseus_of_Macedon_BM.jpg/250px-Perseus_of_Macedon_BM.jpg" height="163" width="170"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/The_Triumph_of_Aemilius_Paulus_%28detail%29.jpg/250px-The_Triumph_of_Aemilius_Paulus_%28detail%29.jpg" height="163" width="245"

Left, a [[ tetradrachm ]] of [[ Perseus of Macedon ]] (r.179–168BC),
[[ British Museum ]]. Right, //The Triumph of
[[ Aemilius Paulus | lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus ]]// (detail) by
[[ Carle Vernet ]], 1789.

Eumenes II came to Rome in 172 BC and delivered a speech to
[[ the Senate | senate-of-the-roman-republic ]] denouncing the alleged crimes
and transgressions of Perseus.[[ ^204 ]] This convinced the Roman Senate to
declare the [[ Third Macedonian War ]] (171–168 BC).[[ ^14 ]] Although
Perseus's forces were victorious against the Romans at the
[[ Battle of Callinicus ]] in 171 BC, the Macedonian army was defeated at the
[[ Battle of Pydna ]] in June 168 BC.[[ ^205 ]] Perseus fled to
[[ Samothrace ]] but surrendered shortly afterwards, was brought to [[ Rome ]]
for the [[ triumph | roman-triumph ]] of
[[ Lucius Aemilius Paullus Macedonicus ]], and was placed under
[[ house arrest ]] at [[ Alba Fucens ]], where he died in 166 BC.[[ ^206 ]]
The Romans abolished the Macedonian monarchy by installing four separate
allied [[ republics | republic ]] in its stead, their capitals located at
[[ Amphipolis ]], [[ Thessalonica ]], [[ Pella ]], and
[[ Pelagonia ]].[[ ^207 ]] The Romans imposed severe laws inhibiting many
social and economic interactions between the inhabitants of these republics,
including the banning of marriages between them and the (temporary)
prohibition on gold and silver mining.[[ ^207 ]] A certain [[ Andriscus ]],
claiming Antigonid descent, rebelled against the Romans and was pronounced
king of Macedonia, defeating the army of the Roman [[ praetor ]] Publius
Juventius Thalna during the [[ Fourth Macedonian War ]]
(150–148 BC).[[ ^208 ]] Despite this, Andriscus was defeated in 148 BC at the
[[ second Battle of Pydna | battle-of-pydna-148-bc ]] by
[[ Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus ]], whose forces occupied the
kingdom.[[ ^209 ]] This was followed in 146 BC by the Roman
[[ destruction of Carthage ]] and victory over the Achaean League at the
[[ Battle of Corinth | battle-of-corinth-146-bc ]], ushering in the era of
[[ Roman Greece ]] and the gradual establishment of the
[[ Roman province of Macedonia ]].[[ ^210 ]]

== Institutions

=== Division of power

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Golden_larnax_and_wreath_of_Philip_II_of_Macedon_at_the_Vergina_museum.jpg/250px-Golden_larnax_and_wreath_of_Philip_II_of_Macedon_at_the_Vergina_museum.jpg" height="165" width="250"

  <figcaption>The [[ Vergina Sun ]], the 16-ray star covering the royal burial [[ larnax ]] of [[ Philip II of Macedon ]] (r. 359–336 BC), discovered in the tomb of [[ Vergina ]], formerly ancient [[ Aigai | aegae-macedonia ]]

At the head of
[[ Macedonia's government | government-of-macedonia-ancient-kingdom ]] was
[[ the king | list-of-macedonian-kings ]] (//[[ basileus ]]//).[[ ^15 ]] From
at least the reign of Philip II, the king was assisted by the
[[ royal pages | royal-page ]] (//basilikoi paides//), bodyguards
(//[[ somatophylakes ]]//), companions (//[[ hetairoi ]]//), friends
(//[[ philoi ]]//), an assembly that included members of the military, and
(during the Hellenistic period) [[ magistrates | magistrate ]].[[ ^211 ]]
Evidence is lacking regarding the extent to which each of these groups shared
authority with the king or if their existence had a basis in a formal
constitutional framework.[[ ^16 ]] Before the reign of Philip II, the only
institution supported by textual evidence is the monarchy.[[ ^17 ]]

=== Kingship and the royal court

The earliest known government of ancient Macedonia was that of its
[[ monarchy ]], lasting until 167 BC when it was abolished by the
Romans.[[ ^212 ]] The Macedonian hereditary monarchy existed since at least
the time of [[ Archaic Greece ]], with Homeric aristocratic roots in
[[ Mycenaean Greece ]].[[ ^213 ]] Thucydides wrote that in previous ages,
Macedonia was divided into small tribal regions, each having its own
[[ petty king ]], the tribes of [[ Lower Macedonia ]] eventually coalescing
under one great king who exercised power as an [[ overlord ]] over the lesser
kings of [[ Upper Macedonia ]].[[ ^19 ]] The direct line of
[[ father-to-son succession | order-of-succession ]] was broken after the
assassination of [[ Orestes of Macedon ]] in 396 BC (allegedly by his
[[ regent ]] and successor [[ Aeropus II of Macedon ]]), clouding the issue of
whether [[ primogeniture ]] was the established custom or if there was a
constitutional right for an assembly of the army or
[[ of the people | popular-assembly ]] to choose another king.[[ ^214 ]] It is
unclear if the male offspring of Macedonian queens or
[[ consorts | queen-consort ]] were always preferred over others given the
accession of [[ Archelaus I of Macedon ]], son of
[[ Perdiccas II of Macedon ]] and a
[[ slave woman | slavery-in-ancient-greece ]], although Archelaus succeeded
the throne after murdering his father's designated
[[ heir apparent ]].[[ ^215 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Hades_abducting_Persephone.jpg/250px-Hades_abducting_Persephone.jpg" height="91" width="250"

  <figcaption>[[ Hades ]] abducting [[ Persephone ]], fresco in the small Macedonian royal tomb at [[ Vergina ]], [[ Macedonia, Greece ]], c. 340 BC

It is known that Macedonian kings before Philip II upheld the privileges and
carried out the responsibilities of hosting foreign diplomats, determining the
kingdom's foreign policies, and negotiating alliances with foreign
powers.[[ ^216 ]] After the Greek victory at [[ Salamis | battle-of-salamis ]]
in 480 BC, the Persian commander [[ Mardonius | mardonius-general ]] had
[[ Alexander I of Macedon ]] sent to Athens as a chief envoy to orchestrate an
alliance between the Achaemenid Empire and [[ Athens | history-of-athens ]].
The decision to send Alexander was based on his
[[ marriage alliance | marriage-of-state ]] with a noble Persian house and his
previous formal relationship with the city-state of Athens.[[ ^216 ]] With
their ownership of natural resources including gold, silver, timber, and
[[ royal land ]], the early Macedonian kings were also capable of
[[ bribing | bribery ]] foreign and domestic parties with impressive
gifts.[[ ^217 ]]

Little is known about the [[ judicial system | judiciary ]] of ancient
Macedonia except that the king acted as the
[[ chief judge | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chief%20judge ]] of the
kingdom.[[ ^218 ]] The Macedonian kings were also
[[ supreme commanders | commander-in-chief ]] of the military.[[ ^18 ]]
Philip II was also highly regarded for his acts of piety in serving as the
[[ high priest ]] of the nation. He performed daily
[[ ritual sacrifices | ritual-sacrifice ]] and led
[[ religious festivals | religious-festival ]].[[ ^219 ]] Alexander imitated
various aspects of his father's reign, such as granting land and gifts to
loyal aristocratic followers,[[ ^219 ]] but lost some core support among them
for adopting some of the trappings of an Eastern, Persian monarch, a "lord and
master" as Carol J. King suggests, instead of a "comrade-in-arms" as was the
traditional relationship of Macedonian kings with their companions.[[ ^220 ]]
Alexander's father, Philip II, was perhaps influenced by Persian traditions
when he adopted institutions similar to those found in the Achaemenid realm,
such as having a [[ royal secretary ]], royal archive, royal pages, and a
seated [[ throne ]].[[ ^221 ]]

=== Royal pages

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Dionysos_on_a_cheetah%2C_Pella%2C_Greece.jpg/250px-Dionysos_on_a_cheetah%2C_Pella%2C_Greece.jpg" height="240" width="249"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Framentuary_votive_relief_representing_a_youth_ladling_wine_for_a_krater_and_a_round_table_with_vases%2C_from_the_Agora%2C_end_of_4th_c._BC%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%287065345633%29.jpg/250px-thumbnail.jpg" height="240" width="180"

Left, the god [[ Dionysos ]] riding a [[ cheetah ]], [[ mosaic ]] floor in the
"House of Dionysos" at [[ Pella ]], Greece, c.330–300BC. Right, a framentary
[[ votive | votive-offering ]][[ relief ]] depicting a youth
[[ ladling | ladle-spoon ]][[ wine | history-of-wine ]] from a
//[[ krater ]]// next to a round table with vases, from the [[ agora ]] of
[[ Pella ]], end of 4thcenturyBC, [[ Archaeological Museum of Pella ]].

The [[ royal pages | royal-page ]] were adolescent boys and young men
[[ conscripted ]] from aristocratic households and serving the kings of
Macedonia perhaps from the reign of Philip II onward, although more solid
evidence dates to the reign of Alexander the Great.[[ ^19 ]] Royal pages
played no direct role in high politics and were conscripted as a means to
introduce them to political life.[[ ^222 ]] After a period of training and
service, pages were expected to become members of the king's companions and
personal retinue.[[ ^223 ]] During their training, pages were expected to
guard the king as he slept, supply him with horses, aid him in mounting his
horse, accompany him on royal hunts, and serve him during
//[[ symposia | symposium-ancient-greece ]]// (i.e. formal drinking
parties).[[ ^224 ]] Although there is little evidence for royal pages in the
Antigonid period, it is known that some of them fled with
[[ Perseus of Macedon ]] to [[ Samothrace ]] following
[[ his defeat | battle-of-pydna ]] by the Romans in 168 BC.[[ ^225 ]]

=== Bodyguards

Royal bodyguards served as the closest members to the king at court and on the
battlefield.[[ ^222 ]] They were split into two categories: the
//[[ agema ]]// of the //[[ hypaspistai ]]//, a type of ancient
[[ special forces ]] usually numbering in the hundreds, and a smaller group of
men handpicked by the king either for their individual merits or to honor the
noble families to which they belonged.[[ ^222 ]] Therefore, the bodyguards,
limited in number and forming the king's inner circle, were not always
responsible for protecting the king's life on and off the battlefield; their
title and office was more a mark of distinction, perhaps used to quell
rivalries between aristocratic houses.[[ ^222 ]]

=== Companions, friends, councils, and assemblies

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/%22An_der_Wende_vom_5._zum_4._Jahrhundert_v.Chr._wurde_Pella_Hauptstadt_des_K%C3%B6nigreichs_Makedonien.%22_6.jpg/250px-%22An_der_Wende_vom_5._zum_4._Jahrhundert_v.Chr._wurde_Pella_Hauptstadt_des_K%C3%B6nigreichs_Makedonien.%22_6.jpg" height="141" width="212"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Fragmentary_inscription_bearing_the_names_of_6_city_archons_%28politarchs%29%2C_2nd_c._BC%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286929923448%29.jpg/250px-Fragmentary_inscription_bearing_the_names_of_6_city_archons_%28politarchs%29%2C_2nd_c._BC%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286929923448%29.jpg" height="141" width="188"

Left, an [[ atrium | atrium-architecture ]] with a pebble-[[ mosaic ]] paving,
in [[ Pella ]], Greece. Right, a fragmentary [[ inscription ]] bearing the
names of six city //[[ archons | archon ]]//
(//[[ politarchs | politarch ]]//), 2ndcenturyBC,
[[ Archaeological Museum of Pella ]].

The companions, including the elite [[ companion cavalry ]] and
//[[ pezhetairoi ]]// infantry, represented a substantially larger group than
the king's bodyguards.[[ ^20 ]] The most trusted or highest ranking companions
formed a council that served as an advisory body to the king.[[ ^226 ]] A
small amount of evidence suggests the existence of an assembly of the army
during times of war and a [[ people's assembly | direct-democracy ]] during
times of peace.[[ ^21 ]]

Members of the council had the right to speak freely, and although there is no
direct evidence that they voted on affairs of state, it is clear that the king
was at least occasionally pressured to agree to their demands.[[ ^227 ]] The
assembly was apparently given the right to judge cases of [[ high treason ]]
and [[ assign punishments | sentence-law ]] for them, such as when Alexander
the Great acted as [[ prosecutor ]] in the trial and conviction of three
alleged conspirators in his father's assassination plot (while many others
[[ were acquitted | acquittal ]]).[[ ^228 ]] However, there is perhaps
insufficient evidence to allow a conclusion that councils and assemblies were
regularly upheld or constitutionally grounded, or that their decisions were
always heeded by the king.[[ ^229 ]] At the death of Alexander the Great, the
companions [[ immediately formed a council | partition-of-babylon ]] to assume
control of his empire, but it was soon destabilized by
[[ open rivalry and conflict | wars-of-the-diadochi ]] between
[[ its members | diadochi ]].[[ ^230 ]] The army also used [[ mutiny ]] as a
tool to achieve political ends.[[ ^22 ]]

=== Magistrates, the commonwealth, local government, and allied states

Antigonid Macedonian kings relied on various regional officials to conduct
affairs of state.[[ ^231 ]] This included high-ranking municipal officials,
such as the military //[[ strategos ]]// and the [[ politarch ]], i.e. the
elected governor (//[[ archon ]]//) of a large city (//[[ polis ]]//), as well
as the politico-religious office of the //[[ epistates ]]//.[[ ^23 ]] No
evidence exists about the personal backgrounds of these officials, although
they may have been chosen among the same group of aristocratic //philoi// and
//hetairoi// who filled vacancies for army officers.[[ ^218 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Tetradrachm%2C_364-363_BC%2C_Amphipolis.jpg/330px-Tetradrachm%2C_364-363_BC%2C_Amphipolis.jpg" height="139" width="280"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Anfipoli%2C_statere_di_filippo_II%2C_340_o_336-328_ac_ca.JPG/250px-Anfipoli%2C_statere_di_filippo_II%2C_340_o_336-328_ac_ca.JPG" height="140" width="150"

Left, a silver [[ tetradrachm ]] issued by the city of [[ Amphipolis ]] in
364–363 BC (before its conquest by [[ Philip II of Macedon ]] in 357 BC),
showing the head of [[ Apollo ]] on the [[ obverse ]] and
[[ racing torch | olympic-flame ]] on the reverse. Right, a golden
[[ stater ]] depicting Philip II, [[ minted | mint-facility ]] at Amphipolis
in 340BC (or later during Alexander's reign), shortly after its conquest by
PhilipII and incorporation into the
[[ Macedonian commonwealth | koinon-of-macedonians ]]

In [[ ancient Athens ]], the [[ Athenian democracy ]] was restored on three
separate occasions following the initial conquest of the city by Antipater in
322 BC.[[ ^232 ]] When it fell repeatedly under Macedonian rule it was
governed by a Macedonian-imposed [[ oligarchy ]] composed of the wealthiest
members of the city-state.[[ ^24 ]] Other city-states were handled quite
differently and were allowed a greater degree of [[ autonomy ]].[[ ^233 ]]
After Philip II conquered Amphipolis in 357 BC, the city was allowed to retain
its [[ democracy ]], including its constitution, [[ popular assembly ]],
[[ city council ]] (//[[ boule | boule-ancient-greece ]]//), and yearly
[[ elections | election ]] for new officials, but a Macedonian garrison was
housed within the city walls along with a Macedonian royal commissioner
(//epistates//) to monitor the city's political affairs.[[ ^234 ]]
[[ Philippi ]], the city founded by Philip II, was the only other city in the
Macedonian [[ commonwealth ]] that had a democratic government with popular
assemblies, since the assembly (//[[ ecclesia | ecclesia-ancient-athens ]]//)
of [[ Thessaloniki ]] seems to have had only a passive function in
practice.[[ ^235 ]] Some cities also maintained their own municipal
[[ revenues | revenue ]].[[ ^233 ]] The Macedonian king and central government
administered the revenues generated by [[ temples | greek-temple ]] and
[[ priesthoods | priesthood ]].[[ ^236 ]]

Within the [[ Macedonian commonwealth | koinon-of-macedonians ]], some
evidence from the 3rd century BC indicates that foreign relations were handled
by the central government. Although individual Macedonian cities nominally
participated in [[ Panhellenic ]] events as independent entities, in reality,
the granting of //[[ asylia ]]// (inviolability, [[ diplomatic immunity ]],
and the [[ right of asylum ]] at [[ sanctuaries ]]) to certain cities was
handled directly by the king.[[ ^237 ]] Likewise, the city-states within
contemporary Greek //[[ koina | koinon ]]// (i.e.,
[[ federations | federation ]] of city-states, the //[[ sympoliteia ]]//)
obeyed the federal decrees [[ voted | vote ]] on collectively by the members
of their league.[[ ^25 ]] In city-states belonging to a league or
commonwealth, the granting of //[[ proxenia ]]// (i.e. the hosting of foreign
ambassadors) was usually a right shared by local and central
authorities.[[ ^238 ]] Abundant evidence exists for the granting of
//proxenia// as being the sole [[ prerogative ]] of central authorities in the
neighbouring [[ Epirote League ]], and some evidence suggests the same
arrangement in the Macedonian commonwealth.[[ ^239 ]] City-states that were
[[ allied | alliance ]] with Macedonia issued their own decrees regarding
//proxenia//.[[ ^240 ]] Foreign leagues also formed alliances with the
Macedonian kings, such as when the [[ Cretan League ]] signed treaties with
[[ Demetrius II Aetolicus ]] and [[ Antigonus III Doson ]] ensuring enlistment
of Cretan mercenaries into the Macedonian army, and elected
[[ Philip V of Macedon ]] as honorary protector (//[[ prostates ]]//) of the
league.[[ ^241 ]]

=== Military

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Macedonian_Army_Pezetairos.jpg/250px-Macedonian_Army_Pezetairos.jpg" height="190" width="160"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82_%D0%BE%D0%B4_%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%87%D0%B5.jpg" height="190" width="178"

Left, a Macedonian infantryman, possibly a [[ hypaspist ]], equipped with an
//[[ aspis ]]// shield and wearing a [[ linothorax ]] cuirass and
[[ Thracian helmet ]]; [[ bas relief ]] from the [[ Alexander Sarcophagus ]],
4thcenturyBC. Right, an ancient Macedonian bronze [[ shield ]] excavated from
the archaeological site at [[ Bonče ]] in [[ North Macedonia ]], dated
4thcenturyBC.

==== Early Macedonian army

The basic structure of the [[ Ancient Macedonian army ]] was the division
between the companion cavalry (//[[ hetairoi ]]//) and the foot companions
(//[[ pezhetairoi ]]//), augmented by various allied troops, foreign levied
soldiers, and mercenaries.[[ ^242 ]] The foot companions existed perhaps since
the reign of [[ Alexander I of Macedon ]].[[ ^243 ]] Macedonian cavalry,
wearing [[ muscled cuirasses | muscled-cuirass ]], became renowned in Greece
during and after their involvement in the [[ Peloponnesian War ]], at times
siding with either Athens or Sparta.[[ ^244 ]] Macedonian infantry in this
period consisted of poorly trained shepherds and farmers, while the cavalry
was composed of noblemen.[[ ^245 ]] As evidenced by early 4th century BC
artwork, there was a pronounced Spartan influence on the Macedonian army
before Philip II.[[ ^246 ]] Nicholas Viktor Sekunda states that at the
beginning of Philip II's reign in 359 BC, the Macedonian army consisted of
10,000 infantry and 600 cavalry,[[ ^247 ]] yet Malcolm Errington cautions that
these figures cited by ancient authors should be treated with some
skepticism.[[ ^248 ]]

==== Philip II and Alexander the Great

After spending years as a political hostage in Thebes, Philip II sought to
imitate the Greek example of [[ martial exercises | military-exercise ]] and
the issuing of
[[ standard equipment | ancient-greek-military-personal-equipment ]] for
citizen soldiery, and succeeded in transforming the Macedonian army from a
levied force of unprofessional farmers into a well-trained,
[[ professional army ]].[[ ^249 ]] Philip II adopted some of the
[[ military tactics ]] of his enemies, such as the //[[ embolon ]]// (flying
wedge) cavalry formation of the [[ Scythians | scythia ]].[[ ^250 ]] His
infantry wielded //peltai// shields that replaced the earlier
//[[ aspis ]]//-style shields, were equipped with
[[ protective helmets | greek-helmet-disambiguation ]],
[[ greaves | greave ]], and either [[ cuirasses | cuirass ]]
[[ breastplates | breastplate ]] or //[[ kotthybos ]]// stomach bands, and
armed with //[[ sarissa ]]// [[ pikes | pike-weapon ]] and
[[ daggers | dagger ]] as secondary weapons.[[ ^26 ]] The elite
//[[ hypaspistai ]]// infantry, composed of handpicked men from the ranks of
the //pezhetairoi//, were formed during the reign of Philip II and saw
continued use during the reign of Alexander the Great.[[ ^251 ]] Philip II was
also responsible for the establishment of the royal bodyguards
(//[[ somatophylakes ]]//).[[ ^252 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Agios_Athanasios_1_fresco.jpg/250px-Agios_Athanasios_1_fresco.jpg" height="90" width="220"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Agios-Athanasios.jpg/250px-Agios-Athanasios.jpg" height="125" width="220"

An ancient fresco of Macedonian soldiers from the tomb of
[[ Agios Athanasios, Thessaloniki ]], Greece, 4th century BC

For his lighter missile troops, Philip II employed mercenary
[[ Cretan archers ]] as well as Thracian, Paeonian, and Illyrian [[ javelin ]]
throwers, [[ slingers | sling-weapon ]], and archers.[[ ^253 ]] He hired
engineers such as [[ Polyidus of Thessaly ]] and [[ Diades of Pella ]], who
were capable of building [[ state of the art ]]
[[ siege engines | siege-engine ]] and [[ artillery ]] that fired large
[[ bolts | crossbow-bolt ]].[[ ^250 ]] Following the acquisition of the
lucrative mines at [[ Krinides ]] (renamed [[ Philippi ]]), the royal treasury
could afford to field a permanent, professional [[ standing army ]].[[ ^254 ]]
The increase in state revenues under Philip II allowed the Macedonians to
build a small navy for the first time, which included
[[ triremes | trireme ]].[[ ^255 ]]

The only Macedonian cavalry units attested under Alexander were the companion
cavalry,[[ ^252 ]] yet he formed a
//[[ hipparchia | hipparchus-cavalry-officer ]]// (i.e. unit of a few hundred
horsemen) of companion cavalry composed entirely of ethnic
[[ Persians | persian-people ]] while campaigning in Asia.[[ ^256 ]] When
marching his forces into Asia, Alexander brought 1,800 cavalrymen from
Macedonia, 1,800 [[ cavalrymen from Thessaly | thessalian-cavalry ]], 600
cavalrymen from the rest of Greece, and 900 //[[ prodromoi ]]// cavalry from
[[ Thrace ]].[[ ^257 ]] Antipater was able to quickly raise a force of 600
native Macedonian cavalry to fight in the [[ Lamian War ]] when it began in
323 BC.[[ ^257 ]] The most elite members of Alexander's //hypaspistai// were
designated as the //[[ agema ]]//, and a new term for //hypaspistai// emerged
after the [[ Battle of Gaugamela ]] in 331 BC: the //[[ argyraspides ]]//
(silver shields).[[ ^258 ]] The latter continued to serve after the reign of
Alexander the Great and may have been of Asian origin.[[ ^27 ]] Overall, his
pike-wielding phalanx infantry numbered some 12,000 men, 3,000 of which were
elite //hypaspistai// and 9,000 of which were //pezhetairoi//.[[ ^28 ]]
Alexander continued the use of Cretan archers and introduced native Macedonian
archers into the army.[[ ^259 ]] After the Battle of Gaugamela, archers of
West Asian backgrounds became commonplace.[[ ^259 ]]

==== Antigonid period military

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Thueros_affresco.jpg/250px-Thueros_affresco.jpg" height="285" width="190"

  <figcaption>Fresco of an ancient Macedonian soldier (//[[ thorakites | thorakitai ]]//) wearing [[ chainmail ]] armor and bearing a [[ thureos ]] shield, 3rd century BC, [[ İstanbul Archaeology Museums ]]

[[ The Macedonian army | antigonid-macedonian-army ]] continued to evolve
under the [[ Antigonid dynasty ]]. It is uncertain how many men were appointed
as //somatophylakes//, which numbered eight men at the end of Alexander the
Great's reign, while the //hypaspistai// seem to have morphed into assistants
of the //somatophylakes//.[[ ^29 ]] At the [[ Battle of Cynoscephalae ]] in
197 BC, the Macedonians commanded some 16,000 phalanx pikemen.[[ ^260 ]]
Alexander the Great's royal squadron of companion cavalry contained 800 men,
the same number of cavalrymen in the sacred squadron
([[ Latin | latin-language ]]: //sacra ala//; [[ Greek | greek-language ]]:
//hiera ile//) commanded by [[ Philip V of Macedon ]] during the
[[ Social War | social-war-220217-bc ]] of 219 BC.[[ ^261 ]] The regular
Macedonian cavalry numbered 3,000 at Callinicus, which was separate from the
sacred squadron and royal cavalry.[[ ^261 ]] While Macedonian cavalry of the
4th century BC had fought without shields, the use of shields by cavalry was
adopted from the [[ Celtic invaders | celtic-settlement-of-eastern-europe ]]
of the 270s BC who settled in [[ Galatia ]], central Anatolia.[[ ^262 ]]

Thanks to [[ contemporary inscriptions | military-decree-of-amphipolis ]] from
Amphipolis and Greia dated 218 and 181 BC, respectively, historians have been
able to partially piece together the organization of the Antigonid army under
Philip V.[[ ^30 ]] From at least the time of [[ Antigonus III Doson ]], the
most elite Antigonid-period infantry were the [[ peltasts | peltast ]],
lighter and more maneuverable soldiers wielding //peltai//
[[ javelins | javelin ]], swords, and a smaller bronze shield than
[[ Macedonian phalanx ]] pikemen, although they sometimes served in that
capacity.[[ ^31 ]] Among the peltasts, roughly 2,000 men were selected to
serve in the elite //agema// [[ vanguard ]], with other peltasts numbering
roughly 3,000.[[ ^263 ]] The number of peltasts varied over time, perhaps
never more than 5,000 men.[[ ^32 ]] They fought alongside the phalanx pikemen,
divided now into //[[ chalkaspides ]]// (bronze shield) and
//[[ leukaspides ]]// (white shield) regiments.[[ ^264 ]]

The Antigonid Macedonian kings continued to expand and equip
[[ the navy | ancient-navies-and-vessels ]].[[ ^265 ]] [[ Cassander ]]
maintained [[ a small fleet | hellenistic-era-warships ]] at [[ Pydna ]],
[[ Demetrius I of Macedon ]] had one at Pella, and [[ Antigonus II Gonatas ]],
while serving as a general for Demetrius in Greece, used the navy to secure
the Macedonian holdings in [[ Demetrias ]], [[ Chalkis ]], [[ Piraeus ]], and
[[ Corinth | ancient-corinth ]].[[ ^266 ]] The navy was considerably expanded
during the [[ Chremonidean War ]] (267–261 BC), allowing the Macedonian navy
to defeat the Ptolemaic Egyptian navy at the 255 BC [[ Battle of Cos ]] and
245 BC [[ Battle of Andros | battle-of-andros-246-bc ]], and enabling
Macedonian influence to spread over the [[ Cyclades ]].[[ ^266 ]]
Antigonus III Doson used the Macedonian navy to invade [[ Caria ]], while
Philip V sent 200 ships to fight in the
[[ Battle of Chios | battle-of-chios-201-bc ]] in 201 BC.[[ ^266 ]] The
Macedonian navy was reduced to a mere six vessels as agreed in the 197 BC
[[ peace treaty ]] that concluded the [[ Second Macedonian War ]] with the
[[ Roman Republic ]], although [[ Perseus of Macedon ]] quickly assembled some
//[[ lemboi ]]// at the outbreak of the [[ Third Macedonian War ]] in
171 BC.[[ ^266 ]]

== Society and culture

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Relief_inscribed_stele%2C_mid_4th_century_B.C.%2C_Archaeological_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg/250px-Relief_inscribed_stele%2C_mid_4th_century_B.C.%2C_Archaeological_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg" height="213" width="160"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Marble_cult_statue_of_Aphrodite_Hypolympidia%2C_from_the_santuary_of_Isis%2C_2nd_c._BC%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Dion_%287079958443%29.jpg/250px-Marble_cult_statue_of_Aphrodite_Hypolympidia%2C_from_the_santuary_of_Isis%2C_2nd_c._BC%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Dion_%287079958443%29.jpg" height="213" width="160"

Left, a Macedonian funerary [[ stele ]], with an [[ epigram ]] in
[[ Greek | greek-language ]], mid-4thcenturyBC, [[ Vergina ]]. Right, marble
cult statue of [[ Aphrodite Hypolympidia ]], dated 2ndcenturyBC, from the
sanctuary of [[ Isis ]] at [[ Dion, Pieria ]], [[ Central Macedonia ]],
Greece, now in the [[ Archaeological Museum of Dion ]].

=== Language and dialects

Following its adoption as the court language of [[ Philip II of Macedon ]]'s
regime, authors of ancient Macedonia wrote their works in [[ Koine Greek ]],
the //[[ lingua franca ]]// of late [[ Classical | classical-greece ]] and
[[ Hellenistic Greece ]].[[ ^33 ]] Rare textual evidence indicates that the
native Macedonian language was either a dialect of
[[ Greek | greek-language ]] similar to [[ Thessalian Greek ]] and
[[ Northwest Greek ]],[[ ^34 ]] or a
[[ language closely related to Greek | hellenic-languages ]].[[ ^35 ]] The
[[ Pella curse tablet ]] has been forwarded as an argument that Ancient
Macedonian was a variety of Northwest Greek.[[ ^267 ]][[ ^268 ]] The vast
majority of surviving inscriptions from ancient Macedonia were written in
[[ Attic Greek ]] and its successor Koine Greek.[[ ^269 ]] Attic (and later
Koine) Greek was the preferred language of the [[ Ancient Macedonian army ]],
although it is known that Alexander the Great once shouted an emergency order
in Macedonian to his royal guards during the
[[ drinking party | symposium-ancient-greece ]] where he killed
[[ Cleitus the Black ]].[[ ^270 ]] Macedonian became
[[ extinct | extinct-language ]] in either the Hellenistic or the Roman
period, and entirely replaced by Koine Greek.[[ ^271 ]][[ ^36 ]]

=== Religious beliefs and funerary practices

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/The_Abduction_of_Persephone_by_Pluto%2C_Amphipolis.jpg/250px-The_Abduction_of_Persephone_by_Pluto%2C_Amphipolis.jpg" height="181" width="250"

  <figcaption>A [[ mosaic ]] of the [[ Kasta Tomb ]] in [[ Amphipolis ]] depicting the abduction of [[ Persephone ]] by [[ Pluto | pluto-mythology ]], 4th century BC

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Lion_of_Amphipolis_BW_2017-10-05_09-38-25.jpg/250px-Lion_of_Amphipolis_BW_2017-10-05_09-38-25.jpg" height="305" width="190"

  <figcaption>The [[ Lion of Amphipolis ]] in [[ Amphipolis ]], [[ northern Greece ]], a 4th-century BC marble tomb sculpture[[ ^272 ]] erected in honor of [[ Laomedon of Mytilene ]], a general who served under [[ Alexander the Great ]]

By the 5th century BC, the Macedonians and the southern Greeks worshiped more
or less the
[[ same deities of the Greek pantheon | list-of-greek-deities ]].[[ ^273 ]] In
Macedonia, political and religious offices were often intertwined. For
instance, the head of state for the city of Amphipolis also served as the
priest of [[ Asklepios ]], Greek god of medicine; a similar arrangement
existed at [[ Cassandreia ]], where a cult priest honoring the city's founder
[[ Cassander ]] was the nominal head of the city.[[ ^274 ]] The main sanctuary
of [[ Zeus ]] was maintained at [[ Dion | dion-pieria ]], while another at
[[ Veria ]] was dedicated to [[ Herakles ]] and was patronized by
[[ Demetrius II Aetolicus ]] (r. 239–229 BC).[[ ^275 ]] Meanwhile, foreign
[[ cults from Egypt | ancient-egyptian-religion ]] were fostered by the royal
court, such as the temple of [[ Sarapis ]] at Thessaloniki.[[ ^276 ]] The
Macedonians also had relations with "international" cults; for example,
Macedonian kings [[ Philip III of Macedon ]] and [[ Alexander IV of Macedon ]]
made [[ votive offerings | votive-offering ]] to the internationally esteemed
[[ Samothrace temple complex ]] of the [[ Cabeiri ]]
[[ mystery cult ]].[[ ^276 ]]

In the three royal tombs at [[ Vergina ]], professional painters decorated the
walls with a mythological scene of [[ Hades ]] abducting [[ Persephone ]] and
royal hunting scenes, while lavish [[ grave goods ]] including
[[ weapons, armor | ancient-greek-military-personal-equipment ]], drinking
vessels, and personal items were housed with the dead, whose bones
[[ were burned | cremation ]] before
[[ burial in golden coffins | ancient-greek-funeral-and-burial-practices ]].[[ ^277 ]]
Some grave goods and decorations were common in other Macedonian tombs, yet
some items found at Vergina were distinctly tied to royalty, including a
[[ diadem ]], luxurious goods, and arms and armor.[[ ^278 ]] Scholars have
debated about the identity of the tomb occupants since
[[ the discovery | manolis-andronikos ]] of their remains in
1977–1978,[[ ^279 ]] and recent research and forensic examination have
concluded that at least one of the persons buried was Philip II.[[ ^37 ]]
Located near Tomb 1 are the above-ground ruins of a //[[ heroon ]]//, a shrine
for [[ cult worship | cult-religion ]] of the dead.[[ ^280 ]] In 2014, the
ancient Macedonian [[ Kasta Tomb ]] was discovered outside of Amphipolis and
is the largest ancient tomb found in Greece (as of 2017).[[ ^281 ]]

=== Economics and social class

Young Macedonian men were typically expected to engage in [[ hunting ]] and
martial combat as a by-product of their [[ transhumance ]] lifestyle of
herding [[ livestock ]] such as goats and sheep, while [[ horse breeding ]]
and raising [[ cattle ]] were other common pursuits.[[ ^282 ]] Some
Macedonians engaged in farming, often with [[ irrigation ]],
[[ land reclamation ]], and [[ horticulture ]] activities supported by the
Macedonian state.[[ ^38 ]] The Macedonian economy and state finances were
mainly supported by [[ logging ]] and by [[ mining ]] valuable
[[ minerals | mineral ]] such as copper, iron, gold, and silver.[[ ^283 ]] The
conversion of these raw materials into finished products and the sale of those
products encouraged the growth of urban centres and a gradual shift away from
the traditional rustic Macedonian lifestyle during the course of the
5th century BC.[[ ^284 ]]

The Macedonian king was an [[ autocratic | autocracy ]] figure at the head of
both government and society, with arguably unlimited authority to handle
affairs of state and public policy, but he was also the leader of a very
personal regime with close relationships or connections to his
//[[ hetairoi ]]//, the core of the Macedonian [[ aristocracy ]].[[ ^285 ]]
These aristocrats were second only to the king in terms of power and
privilege, filling the ranks of his administration and serving as commanding
officers in the military.[[ ^286 ]] It was in the more bureaucratic regimes of
the [[ Hellenistic kingdoms ]] that succeeded Alexander the Great's empire
where greater [[ social mobility ]] for members of society seeking to join the
aristocracy could be found, especially in Ptolemaic Egypt.[[ ^287 ]] Although
governed by a king and martial aristocracy, Macedonia seems to have lacked the
widespread [[ use of slaves | history-of-slavery ]] seen in contemporaneous
Greek states.[[ ^288 ]]

=== Visual arts

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Fresco_of_a_Macedonian_soldier%2C_from_the_Tomb_of_Agios_Athanasios%2C_4th_century_BC.jpg/250px-Fresco_of_a_Macedonian_soldier%2C_from_the_Tomb_of_Agios_Athanasios%2C_4th_century_BC.jpg" height="275" width="145"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_The_Tomb_of_Jugdement_545fddcedb8f434cdb346f41dbd838ec.jpg/250px-Ancient_Mieza%2C_Macedonian_tombs_of_Lefkadia%2C_The_Tomb_of_Jugdement_545fddcedb8f434cdb346f41dbd838ec.jpg" height="274" width="150"

Left, a fresco of a [[ Macedonian soldier | ancient-macedonian-army ]] resting
a spear and [[ wearing a cap | kausia ]], from the tomb of
[[ Agios Athanasios, Thessaloniki ]], 4thcenturyBC. Right, fresco from the
[[ Tomb of Judgement | tomb-of-judgement-lefkadia ]] in ancient
[[ Mieza | mieza-macedonia ]] (modern-day Lefkadia), [[ Imathia ]],
[[ Central Macedonia ]], Greece, depicting religious imagery of
[[ the afterlife | greek-underworld ]], 4thcenturyBC.

By the reign of [[ Archelaus I | archelaus-i-of-macedon ]] in the 5th century
BC, the ancient Macedonian elite was importing customs and artistic traditions
from other regions of Greece while retaining more archaic, perhaps
[[ Homeric | homer ]], funerary rites connected with the
[[ symposium | symposium-ancient-greece ]] that were typified by items such as
the decorative metal [[ kraters | krater ]] that held the ashes of deceased
Macedonian nobility in their tombs.[[ ^289 ]] Among these is the large bronze
[[ Derveni Krater ]] from a 4th-century BC tomb of Thessaloniki, decorated
with scenes of the Greek god [[ Dionysus ]] and
[[ his entourage | cult-of-dionysus ]] and belonging to an aristocrat who had
had a military career.[[ ^290 ]] Macedonian [[ metalwork ]] usually followed
[[ Athenian styles of vase shapes | pottery-of-ancient-greece ]] from the
6th century BC onward, with drinking vessels, jewellery, containers, crowns,
[[ diadems | diadem ]], and [[ coins | ancient-greek-coinage ]] among the many
metal objects found in Macedonian tombs.[[ ^291 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg/250px-Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg" height="113" width="250"

  <figcaption>[[ Alexander | alexander-the-great ]] (left), wearing a //[[ kausia ]]// and fighting an [[ Asiatic lion ]] with his friend [[ Craterus ]] (detail); late 4th-century BC [[ mosaic ]],[[ ^292 ]] [[ Pella ]] Museum.

Surviving Macedonian painted artwork includes [[ frescoes | fresco ]] and
[[ murals | mural ]], but also decoration on
[[ sculpted artwork | ancient-greek-sculpture ]] such as
[[ statues | statue ]] and [[ reliefs | relief ]]. For instance, trace colours
still exist on the [[ bas-reliefs | bas-relief ]] of the late 4th-century BC
[[ Alexander Sarcophagus ]].[[ ^293 ]] Macedonian paintings have allowed
historians to investigate the clothing fashions as well as military gear worn
by the [[ ancient Macedonians ]].[[ ^294 ]] Aside from metalwork and painting,
[[ mosaics | mosaic ]] are another significant form of surviving Macedonian
artwork.[[ ^291 ]] The [[ Stag Hunt Mosaic ]] of Pella, with its
three-dimensional qualities and illusionist style, show clear influence from
painted artwork and wider Hellenistic art trends, although the rustic theme of
hunting was tailored to Macedonian tastes.[[ ^295 ]] The similar Lion Hunt
Mosaic of Pella illustrates either a scene of Alexander the Great with his
companion [[ Craterus ]], or simply a conventional illustration of the royal
diversion of hunting.[[ ^295 ]] Mosaics with mythological themes include
scenes of Dionysus riding a panther and [[ Helen of Troy ]] being abducted by
[[ Theseus ]], the latter of which employs illusionist qualities and realistic
shading similar to Macedonian paintings.[[ ^295 ]] Common themes of Macedonian
paintings and mosaics include warfare, hunting, and aggressive masculine
sexuality (i.e. abduction of women for rape or marriage); these subjects are
at times combined within a single work and perhaps indicate a metaphorical
connection.[[ ^39 ]]

=== Theatre, music and performing arts

Philip II was assassinated in 336 BC at the theatre of
[[ Aigai | aegae-macedonia ]], amid games and spectacles celebrating the
marriage of his daughter [[ Cleopatra | cleopatra-of-macedon ]].[[ ^296 ]]
Alexander the Great was allegedly a great admirer of both theatre and
music.[[ ^297 ]] He was especially fond of the [[ plays | play-theatre ]] by
[[ Classical Athenian ]] [[ tragedians | tragedian ]] [[ Aeschylus ]],
[[ Sophocles ]], and [[ Euripides ]], whose works formed part of a proper
[[ Greek education | education-in-ancient-greece ]] for his new eastern
subjects alongside studies in the Greek language, including the
[[ epics | epic-cycle ]] of [[ Homer ]].[[ ^298 ]] While he and his army were
stationed at [[ Tyre | tyre-lebanon ]] (in modern-day Lebanon), Alexander had
his generals act as judges not only for athletic contests but also for stage
performances of Greek tragedies.[[ ^299 ]] The contemporaneous famous actors
[[ Thessalus | thessalus-actor ]] and Athenodorus performed at the
event.[[ ^40 ]]

[[ Music | history-of-music ]] was also appreciated in Macedonia. In addition
to the [[ agora ]], the [[ gymnasium | gymnasium-ancient-greece ]], the
[[ theatre ]], and [[ religious sanctuaries | religious-sanctuary ]] and
[[ temples | ancient-greek-temple ]] dedicated to Greek gods and goddesses,
one of the main markers of a true Greek city in the
[[ empire of Alexander the Great ]] was the presence of an
[[ odeon | odeon-building ]] for
[[ musical performances | concert ]].[[ ^300 ]] This was the case not only for
[[ Alexandria ]] in [[ Egypt | history-of-egypt ]], but also for cities as
distant as [[ Ai-Khanoum ]] in what is now modern-day
[[ Afghanistan | history-of-afghanistan ]].[[ ^300 ]]

=== Literature, education, philosophy, and patronage

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg/250px-Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg" height="253" width="190"

  <figcaption>[[ Portrait bust | bust-sculpture ]] of [[ Aristotle ]], an [[ Imperial Roman | roman-empire ]] (1st or 2nd century AD) copy of a lost [[ bronze sculpture ]] made by [[ Lysippos ]]

[[ Perdiccas II of Macedon ]] was able to host well-known Classical Greek
intellectual visitors at his royal court, such as the lyric poet
[[ Melanippides ]] and the renowned medical doctor [[ Hippocrates ]], and
[[ Pindar ]]'s //[[ enkomion | encomium ]]// written for
[[ Alexander I of Macedon ]] may have been composed at his court.[[ ^301 ]]
Archelaus I received many more Greek scholars, artists, and celebrities at his
court than his predecessors.[[ ^302 ]] His honored guests included the
[[ painter | history-of-painting#egypt-greece-and-rome ]]
[[ Zeuxis | zeuxis-painter ]], the
[[ architect | ancient-greek-architecture ]]
[[ Callimachus | callimachus-sculptor ]], the poets [[ Choerilus of Samos ]],
[[ Timotheus of Miletus ]], and [[ Agathon ]], as well as the famous Athenian
[[ playwright ]] [[ Euripides ]].[[ ^41 ]] The philosopher [[ Aristotle ]],
who studied at the [[ Platonic Academy ]] of Athens and established the
[[ Aristotelian school of thought | aristotelianism ]], moved to Macedonia,
and is said to have tutored the young Alexander the Great, as well as serving
as an esteemed diplomat for Philip II.[[ ^303 ]] Among Alexander's retinue of
artists, writers, and philosophers was [[ Pyrrho of Elis ]], founder of
[[ Pyrrhonism ]], the school of [[ philosophical skepticism ]].[[ ^298 ]]
During the Antigonid period, [[ Antigonos Gonatas ]] fostered cordial
relationships with [[ Menedemos of Eretria ]], founder of the
[[ Eretrian school ]] of philosophy, and [[ Zenon | zeno-of-citium ]], the
founder of [[ Stoicism ]].[[ ^297 ]]

In terms of early [[ Greek historiography ]] and later
[[ Roman historiography ]], [[ Felix Jacoby ]] identified thirteen possible
ancient [[ historians | list-of-greek-historiographers ]] who wrote about
Macedonia in his //[[ Fragmente der griechischen Historiker ]]//.[[ ^304 ]]
Aside from accounts in [[ Herodotus ]] and Thucydides, the works compiled by
Jacoby are only fragmentary, whereas other works are completely lost, such as
the history of an [[ Illyrian | illyria ]] war fought by
[[ Perdiccas III | perdiccas-iii-of-macedon ]] written by Antipater.[[ ^305 ]]
The Macedonian historians [[ Marsyas of Pella ]] and [[ Marsyas of Philippi ]]
wrote histories of Macedonia, the [[ Ptolemaic | ptolemaic-egypt ]] king
[[ Ptolemy I Soter ]] authored a history about Alexander, and
[[ Hieronymus of Cardia ]] wrote a history about Alexander's royal
successors.[[ ^42 ]] Following the
[[ Indian campaign of Alexander the Great ]], the Macedonian military officer
[[ Nearchus ]] wrote a work of his [[ voyage | travel-literature ]] from the
mouth of the [[ Indus river ]] to the [[ Persian Gulf ]].[[ ^306 ]] The
Macedonian [[ historian Craterus | craterus-historian ]] published a
compilation of decrees made by
[[ the popular assembly | ecclesia-ancient-athens ]] of the
[[ Athenian democracy ]], ostensibly while attending the school of
Aristotle.[[ ^306 ]] [[ Philip V of Macedon ]] had manuscripts of the history
of Philip II written by [[ Theopompus ]] gathered by his court scholars and
disseminated with further copies.[[ ^297 ]]

=== Sports and leisure

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Hades_and_Persephone%2C_Vergina.jpg/250px-Hades_and_Persephone%2C_Vergina.jpg" height="143" width="250"

  <figcaption>A fresco showing [[ Hades ]] and [[ Persephone ]] riding in a [[ chariot ]], from the tomb of Queen [[ Eurydice I of Macedon ]] at [[ Vergina ]], Greece, 4th century BC

When Alexander I of Macedon petitioned to compete in the [[ foot race ]] of
the ancient Olympic Games, the event organizers at first denied his request,
explaining that only Greeks were allowed to compete. However, Alexander I
produced proof of an Argead royal [[ genealogy ]] showing ancient [[ Argive ]]
[[ Temenid ]] lineage, a move that ultimately convinced the Olympic
//[[ Hellanodikai ]]// authorities of his Greek descent and ability to
compete.[[ ^307 ]] By the end of the 5th century BC, the Macedonian king
Archelaus I was crowned with the [[ olive wreath ]] at both
[[ Olympia | olympia-greece ]] and [[ Delphi ]] (in the [[ Pythian Games ]])
for winning [[ chariot racing ]] contests.[[ ^308 ]] Philip II allegedly heard
of the Olympic victory of his horse (in either an individual [[ horse race ]]
or chariot race) on the same day his son Alexander the Great was born, on
either 19 or 20 July 356 BC.[[ ^309 ]] Non-royal Macedonians also competed in
and won various Olympic contests by the 4th century BC.[[ ^310 ]] In addition
to literary contests, Alexander the Great staged
[[ competitions for music | music-competition ]] and athletics across his
empire.[[ ^298 ]]

=== Dining and cuisine

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Banquet%2C_tombe_d%27Agios_Athanasios.jpg/250px-Banquet%2C_tombe_d%27Agios_Athanasios.jpg" height="73" width="250"

  <figcaption>A [[ banquet ]] scene from a Macedonian tomb of [[ Agios Athanasios, Thessaloniki ]], 4th century BC; shown are six men [[ reclining on couches | symposium-ancient-greece ]], with food arranged on nearby tables, a male servant in attendance, and female musicians providing entertainment.[[ ^311 ]]

Ancient Macedonia produced only a few fine foods or beverages that were highly
appreciated elsewhere in the Greek world, including [[ eels | eel ]] from the
[[ Strymonian Gulf ]] and special [[ wine | history-of-wine ]] produced in
[[ Chalcidice ]].[[ ^312 ]] The earliest known use of flat bread as a plate
for meat was made in Macedonia during the 3rd century BC, which perhaps
influenced the later [[ trencher bread | trencher-tableware ]] of
[[ medieval Europe ]].[[ ^312 ]] [[ Cattle ]] and [[ goats | goat ]] were
consumed, although there was no notice of Macedonian mountain
[[ cheeses | history-of-cheese ]] in literature until the
[[ Middle Ages ]].[[ ^312 ]] The comedic playwright [[ Menander ]] wrote that
Macedonian dining habits penetrated [[ Athenian | history-of-athens ]] high
society; for instance, the introduction of meats into the [[ dessert ]] course
of a meal.[[ ^313 ]] The Macedonians also most likely introduced //mattye// to
Athenian cuisine, a dish usually made of chicken or other spiced, salted, and
sauced meats served
[[ during the wine course | full-course-dinner ]].[[ ^314 ]] This particular
dish was derided and connected with licentiousness and drunkenness in a play
by the Athenian comic poet [[ Alexis | alexis-poet ]] about the declining
morals of Athenians in the age of [[ Demetrius I of Macedon ]].[[ ^315 ]]

The //[[ symposium | symposium-ancient-greece ]]// in the Macedonian and wider
Greek realm was a banquet for the nobility and privileged class, an occasion
for feasting, drinking, entertainment, and sometimes
[[ philosophical discussion | symposium-plato ]].[[ ^316 ]] The
//[[ hetairoi ]]//, leading members of the Macedonian [[ aristocracy ]], were
expected to attend such feasts with their king.[[ ^286 ]] They were also
expected to accompany him on royal hunts for the acquisition of
[[ game meat ]] as well as for sport.[[ ^286 ]]

=== Ethnic identity

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Macedonian_boy_BM_1906.10-19.1.jpg/120px-Macedonian_boy_BM_1906.10-19.1.jpg" height="209" width="94"

Athenian terracotta figurine, c.300BC.

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Terrakota_Statue_eines_Makedoniers_3_Jhdt_v_Chr.jpg/120px-Terrakota_Statue_eines_Makedoniers_3_Jhdt_v_Chr.jpg" height="208" width="90"

Macedonian terracotta figurine, 3rd centuryBC[[ Terracotta ]] statues
depicting [[ ancient Macedonians ]] wearing the //[[ kausia ]]//, a headgear
that led the [[ Persians ]] to refer to the Macedonians as "Yaunã Takabara"
("Greeks with hats that look like shields").[[ ^317 ]]

Ancient authors and modern scholars alike disagree about the precise ethnic
identity of the ancient Macedonians. The predominant viewpoint supports that
the Macedonians were "truly Greeks" who had just retained a more archaic
lifestyle than those living in southern parts of Greece.[[ ^318 ]]
[[ Ernst Badian ]] notes however that nearly all surviving references to
antagonisms and differences between Greeks and Macedonians exist in the
written speeches of [[ Arrian ]], who lived at the time of the
[[ Roman Empire ]], when any notion of an ethnic disparity between Macedonians
and other Greeks was incomprehensible.[[ ^319 ]] Hatzopoulos argues that there
was no real ethnic difference between Macedonians and the other Greeks, only a
political distinction contrived after the creation of the
[[ League of Corinth ]] in 337 BC (which was led by Macedonia through the
league's elected //[[ hegemon ]]// Philip II, when he was not a member of the
league itself);[[ ^43 ]] [[ N. G. L. Hammond ]] asserts that ancient views
differentiating Macedonia's ethnic identity from the rest of the
Greek-speaking world should be seen as an expression of conflict between two
different political systems: the democratic system of the city-states (e.g.
Athens) versus the monarchy (Macedonia).[[ ^320 ]] Other academics who concur
that the difference between the Macedonians and Greeks was a political rather
than a true ethnic discrepancy include Michael B. Sakellariou,[[ ^321 ]]
Malcolm Errington,[[ ^44 ]] and Craige B. Champion.[[ ^45 ]]

Anson argues that some Hellenic authors expressed complex or even
ever-changing and ambiguous ideas about the exact ethnic identity of the
Macedonians, who were considered by some as barbarians and others as
semi-Greek or fully Greek.[[ ^46 ]] Roger D. Woodard asserts that in addition
to persisting uncertainty in modern times about the proper classification of
the Macedonian language and its relation to Greek, ancient authors also
presented conflicting ideas about the Macedonians.[[ ^47 ]]
[[ Simon Hornblower ]] argues on the Greek identity of the Macedonians, taking
into consideration their origin, language, cults and customs related to
ancient Greek traditions.[[ ^322 ]] Any preconceived ethnic differences
between Greeks and Macedonians faded by 148 BC soon after the
[[ Roman conquest of Macedonia | macedonian-wars ]] and then
[[ the rest of Greece | macedonia-roman-province ]] with the defeat of the
[[ Achaean League ]] by the [[ Roman Republic ]] at the
[[ Battle of Corinth (146 BC) ]].[[ ^323 ]]

== Technology and engineering

=== Architecture

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/The_facade_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263674714%29.jpg/250px-The_facade_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263674714%29.jpg" height="225" width="169"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/The_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes_%28photography_of_the_pediment%29%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263729086%29.jpg/250px-The_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes_%28photography_of_the_pediment%29%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263729086%29.jpg" height="225" width="165"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_facade_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263705128%29.jpg/250px-The_facade_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263705128%29.jpg" height="126" width="164"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/The_pediment_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263694348%29.jpg/250px-The_pediment_of_the_the_Tomb_of_the_Palmettes%2C_first_half_of_the_3rd_century_BC%2C_Ancient_Mieza_%287263694348%29.jpg" height="125" width="170"

The [[ facade ]] of the Macedonian [[ Tomb of the Palmettes ]] in
[[ Mieza, Macedonia ]], [[ Greece ]], 3rd century BC; decorated by coloured
[[ Doric | doric-order ]] and [[ Ionic | ionic-order ]] mouldings, the
[[ pediment ]] is also painted with a scene of a man and woman reclining
together.[[ ^324 ]]

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Fragment_of_painted_roof_tiles_%28raking%2C_simas%2C_pan-tiles%29%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286919206262%29.jpg/250px-Fragment_of_painted_roof_tiles_%28raking%2C_simas%2C_pan-tiles%29%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286919206262%29.jpg" height="134" width="245"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Ionic_pilaster_capital_from_the_palace%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286930003102%29.jpg/250px-Ionic_pilaster_capital_from_the_palace%2C_Archaeological_Museum%2C_Pella_%286930003102%29.jpg" height="133" width="175"

Left, fragments of ancient Macedonian painted [[ roof tiles | roof-tile ]]
(raking, simas, pan-tiles), [[ Archaeological Museum of Pella ]], Greece.
Right, the [[ Ionic capital ]] of a [[ pilaster ]] from the [[ palace ]] at
[[ Pella ]], [[ Archaeological Museum of Pella ]].

Macedonian architecture, although utilizing a mixture of different forms and
styles from the rest of Greece, did not represent a unique or diverging style
from other
[[ ancient Greek architecture | architecture-of-ancient-greece ]].[[ ^295 ]]
Among the [[ classical orders | classical-order ]], Macedonian architects
favoured the [[ Ionic order ]], especially in the [[ peristyle ]] courtyards
of private homes.[[ ^325 ]] There are several surviving examples, albeit in
ruins, of Macedonian palatial architecture, including a [[ palace ]] at the
site of the capital Pella, the summer residence of [[ Vergina ]] near the old
capital Aigai, and the royal residence at [[ Demetrias ]] near modern
[[ Volos ]].[[ ^325 ]] At Vergina, the ruins of three large
[[ banquet halls | banquet-hall ]] with marble-[[ tiled | tile ]] floors
(covered in the debris of [[ roof tiles ]]) with floor plan dimensions
measuring roughly 16.7 x 17.6 m (54.8 x 57.7 ft) demonstrate perhaps the
earliest examples of monumental
[[ triangular roof trusses | timber-roof-trusses ]], if dated before the reign
of [[ Antigonus II Gonatas ]] or even the onset of the Hellenistic
period.[[ ^326 ]] Later Macedonian architecture also featured
[[ arches | arch ]] and [[ vaults | vault-architecture ]].[[ ^327 ]] The
palaces of both Vergina and Demetrias had walls made of sundried
[[ bricks | brick ]], while the latter palace had four corner
[[ towers | tower ]] around a central courtyard in the manner of a fortified
residence fit for a king or at least a military governor.[[ ^325 ]]

Macedonian rulers also sponsored works of architecture outside of Macedonia
proper. For instance, following his victory at the
[[ Battle of Chaeronea (338 BC) ]], Philip II raised a round memorial building
at [[ Olympia | olympia-greece ]] known as the [[ Philippeion ]], decorated
inside with statues depicting him, his parents [[ Amyntas III of Macedon ]]
and [[ Eurydice I of Macedon ]], his wife [[ Olympias ]], and his son
Alexander the Great.[[ ^328 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/20100913_Ancient_Theater_Marwneia_Rhodope_Greece_panoramic_3.jpg/250px-20100913_Ancient_Theater_Marwneia_Rhodope_Greece_panoramic_3.jpg" height="100" width="250"

  <figcaption>Ruins of the [[ ancient theatre | ancient-greek-theatre ]] in [[ Maroneia ]], [[ Rhodope | rhodope-regional-unit ]], [[ East Macedonia and Thrace ]], Greece

The ruins of roughly twenty [[ Greek theatres | greek-theatre ]] survive in
the present-day
[[ regions of Macedonia and Thrace in Greece | decentralized-administration-of-macedonia-and-thrace ]]:
sixteen open-air theatres, three [[ odea | odeon-building ]], and a possible
theatre in [[ Veria ]] undergoing excavation.[[ ^329 ]]

=== Military technology and engineering

By the Hellenistic period, it became common for Greek states to finance the
development and proliferation of ever more powerful
[[ torsion siege engines | torsion-siege-engine ]],
[[ naval ships | naval-warfare ]], and standardized designs for
[[ arms and armor | ancient-greek-military-personal-equipment ]].[[ ^330 ]]
Under Philip II and Alexander the Great, improvements were made to
[[ siege artillery ]] such as bolt-shooting [[ ballistae | ballista ]] and
[[ siege engines | siege-engine ]] such as huge rolling
[[ siege towers | siege-tower ]].[[ ^331 ]] E. W. Marsden and M. Y. Treister
contend that the Macedonian rulers [[ Antigonus I Monophthalmus ]] and his
successor [[ Demetrius I of Macedon ]] had the most powerful siege artillery
of the Hellenistic world at the end of the 4th century BC.[[ ^332 ]]
[[ The siege | battle-of-salamis-306-bc ]] of [[ Salamis, Cyprus ]], in 306 BC
necessitated the building of large siege engines and drafting of craftsmen
from parts of [[ West Asia ]].[[ ^333 ]] The siege tower commissioned by
Demetrius I for the Macedonian [[ Siege of Rhodes (305–304 BC) ]] and defended
by over three thousand soldiers was built at a height of nine
[[ stories | storey ]].[[ ^334 ]] It had a base of 4,300 square feet (399
square metres), eight wheels that were steered in either direction by pivots,
three sides covered in iron plates to protect them from fire, and mechanically
opened windows (shielded with wool-stuffed leather curtains to soften the blow
of ballistae rounds) of different sizes to accommodate the firing of missiles
ranging from arrows to larger bolts.[[ ^334 ]]

During the siege of [[ Echinus | echinus-phthiotis ]] by
[[ Philip V of Macedon ]] in 211 BC, the besiegers built
[[ tunnels | mining-military ]] to protect the soldiers and
[[ sappers | sapper ]] as they went back and forth from the camp to the siege
works. These included two siege towers connected by a makeshift
[[ wickerwork ]] [[ curtain wall | curtain-wall-fortification ]] mounted with
stone-shooting ballistae, and sheds to protect the approach of the
[[ battering ram ]].[[ ^335 ]] Despite the early reputation of Macedon as a
leader in siege technology, [[ Alexandria ]] in [[ Ptolemaic Egypt ]] became
the centre for technological improvements to the [[ catapult ]] by the
3rd century BC, as evidenced by the writings of
[[ Philo of Alexandria ]].[[ ^333 ]]

=== Other innovations

Although perhaps not as prolific as other areas of Greece in regards to
technological innovations, there are some inventions that may have originated
in Macedonia aside from siege engines and artillery. The
[[ rotary-operated | rotation-around-a-fixed-axis ]] [[ olive press ]] for
producing [[ olive oil ]] may have been invented in ancient Macedonia or
another part of Greece, or even as far east as the [[ Levant ]] or
[[ Anatolia ]].[[ ^336 ]] [[ Mould-pressed glass | history-of-glass ]] first
appeared in Macedonia in the 4th century BC (although it could have
simultaneously existed in the Achaemenid Empire); the first known clear,
translucent glass pieces of the Greek world have been discovered in Macedonia
and [[ Rhodes ]] and date to the second half of the 4th century BC.[[ ^337 ]]
Greek technical and [[ scientific literature ]] began with
[[ Classical Athens ]] in the 5th century BC, while the major production
centres for technical innovation and texts during the Hellenistic period were
[[ Alexandria | library-of-alexandria ]], [[ Rhodes | rhodian-school ]], and
[[ Pergamon ]].[[ ^338 ]]

== Currency, finances, and resources

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Mints_of_Alexander_the_Great_1.jpg/250px-Mints_of_Alexander_the_Great_1.jpg" height="329" width="250"

  <figcaption>[[ Tetradrachms | tetradrachm ]] (above) and [[ drachms | drachm ]] (below) issued during the reign of [[ Alexander the Great ]], now in the [[ Numismatic Museum of Athens ]]

The [[ minting | mint-facility ]] of silver coinage began during the reign of
[[ Alexander I | alexander-i-of-macedon ]] as a means to pay for royal
expenditures.[[ ^218 ]] [[ Archelaus I | archelaus-i-of-macedon ]] increased
the silver content of his coins as well as minting copper coins to promote
foreign and domestic commerce.[[ ^47 ]] The minting of coinage significantly
increased during the reigns of Philip II and Alexander the Great, especially
after the increase in state revenues following the seizure of the
[[ Pangaion Hills ]].[[ ^339 ]] During the Hellenistic period the royal houses
of Macedonia, [[ Ptolemaic Egypt ]], and the [[ Attalid kingdom ]] exercised
[[ full monopolistic control | state-monopoly ]] over [[ mining ]] activities,
largely to ensure the funding of their armies.[[ ^340 ]] By the end of the
[[ conquests of Alexander the Great ]], nearly thirty mints stretching from
Macedonia to [[ Babylon ]] produced standard coins.[[ ^341 ]] The right to
mint coins was shared by [[ central | central-government ]] and some
[[ local governments | local-government ]], i.e. the [[ autonomous ]]
[[ municipal governments | municipal-government ]] of Thessaloniki, Pella, and
Amphipolis within the Macedonian [[ commonwealth ]].[[ ^342 ]] The Macedonians
were also the first to issue different coins for
[[ internal and external circulation | circulation-currency ]].[[ ^343 ]]

State revenues were also raised by collecting [[ produce ]] from
[[ arable lands | arable-land ]], timber from forests, and
[[ taxes | tax#history ]] on [[ imports | import ]] and [[ exports | export ]]
at [[ harbors | harbor ]].[[ ^344 ]] Some mines, [[ groves | grove-nature ]],
[[ agricultural lands | history-of-agriculture ]], and [[ forests | logging ]]
belonging to the Macedonian state were exploited by the Macedonian king,
although these were often [[ leased | lease ]] as [[ assets | asset ]] or
given as [[ grants | grant-money ]] to members of the [[ nobility ]] such as
the //[[ hetairoi ]]// and //[[ philoi ]]//.[[ ^345 ]] [[ Tariffs | tariff ]]
exacted on goods flowing in and out of Macedonian [[ seaports | seaport ]]
existed from at least the reign of [[ Amyntas III | amyntas-iii-of-macedon ]],
and [[ Callistratus of Aphidnae ]] (d. c. 350 BC) aided
[[ Perdiccas III | perdiccas-iii-of-macedon ]] in doubling the kingdom's
annual profits on [[ customs duties ]] from 20 to 40
[[ talents | talent-measurement ]].[[ ^346 ]]

After the defeat of [[ Perseus | perseus-of-macedon ]] at
[[ Pydna | battle-of-pydna ]] in 168 BC, the [[ Roman Senate ]] allowed the
reopening of iron and copper mines, but forbade the mining of gold and silver
by the four newly established autonomous [[ client states | client-state ]]
that replaced the monarchy in Macedonia.[[ ^347 ]] The law may originally have
been conceived by the Senate due to the fear that material wealth gained from
gold and silver mining operations would allow the Macedonians to fund an armed
rebellion.[[ ^348 ]] The Romans were perhaps also concerned with stemming
[[ inflation ]] caused by an increased [[ money supply ]] from Macedonian
silver mining.[[ ^349 ]] The Macedonians continued minting silver coins
between 167 and 148 BC (i.e. just before the establishment of the
[[ Roman province of Macedonia ]]), and when the Romans lifted the ban on
Macedonian silver mining in 158 BC it may simply have reflected the local
reality of this illicit practice continuing regardless of the Senate's
decree.[[ ^350 ]]

== Legacy

The reigns of Philip II and Alexander the Great witnessed the demise of
Classical Greece and the birth of Hellenistic civilization, following the
[[ spread of Greek culture | hellenization ]] to the [[ Near East ]] during
and after Alexander's conquests.[[ ^351 ]] Macedonians then migrated to Egypt
and parts of Asia, but the intensive [[ colonization ]] of foreign lands
sapped the available manpower in Macedonia proper, weakening the kingdom in
its fight with other Hellenistic powers and contributing to its downfall and
conquest by the Romans.[[ ^352 ]] However, the diffusion of Greek culture and
language cemented by Alexander's conquests in West Asia and North Africa
served as a "precondition" for the [[ later Roman expansion | rise-of-rome ]]
into these territories and [[ entire basis | byzantine-greeks ]] for the
[[ Byzantine Empire ]], according to Errington.[[ ^353 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Battle_of_Issos_MAN_Napoli_Inv10020_n01.jpg/250px-Battle_of_Issos_MAN_Napoli_Inv10020_n01.jpg" height="117" width="250"

  <figcaption>The //[[ Alexander Mosaic ]]//, a [[ Roman mosaic ]] from [[ Pompeii ]], Italy, c. 100 BC

The ethnic Macedonian rulers of the Ptolemaic and Seleucid successor states
accepted men from all over the Greek world as their //hetairoi// companions
and did not foster a national identity like the Antigonids.[[ ^354 ]] Modern
scholarship has focused on how these Hellenistic successor kingdoms were
influenced more by their Macedonian origins than Eastern or southern Greek
traditions.[[ ^355 ]] While [[ Spartan society | spartan-constitution ]]
remained mostly insular and Athens continued placing
[[ strict limitations on acquiring citizenship | solonian-constitution ]], the
[[ cosmopolitan | cosmopolitanism ]] Hellenistic cities of Asia and
northeastern Africa bore a greater resemblance to Macedonian cities and
contained a mixture of subjects including natives, Greek and Macedonian
colonists, and Greek-speaking Hellenized Easterners, many of whom were the
product of intermarriage between Greeks and native populations.[[ ^356 ]]

The [[ deification ]] of Macedonian monarchs perhaps began with the death of
Philip II, but it was his son Alexander the Great who unambiguously claimed to
be a [[ living god | imperial-cult ]].[[ ^48 ]] Following his visit to the
[[ oracle ]] of [[ Didyma ]] in 334 BC that suggested his divinity, Alexander
traveled to the [[ Oracle | oracle-of-ammon ]] of [[ Zeus Ammon ]]—the
[[ Greek equivalent | interpretatio-graeca ]] of the Egyptian [[ Amun-Ra ]]—at
the [[ Siwa Oasis ]] of the [[ Libyan Desert ]] in 332 BC to confirm his
[[ divine status | sacred-king ]].[[ ^49 ]] Although the Ptolemaic and
Seleucid empires
[[ maintained ancestral cults | ptolemaic-cult-of-alexander-the-great ]] and
deified their rulers, kings were not worshiped in the Kingdom of
Macedonia.[[ ^357 ]] While Zeus Ammon was known to the Greeks prior to
Alexander's reign, particularly at the
[[ Greek colony | colonies-in-antiquity ]] of [[ Cyrene, Libya ]], Alexander
was the first Macedonian monarch to patronize
[[ Egyptian | egyptian-mythology ]], [[ Persian | persian-mythology ]], and
[[ Babylonian priesthoods and deities | babylonian-religion ]], strengthening
the fusion of [[ Near Eastern | ancient-mesopotamian-religion ]] and Greek
religious beliefs.[[ ^358 ]] After his reign, the
[[ cult of Isis | mysteries-of-isis ]] gradually spread throughout the
Hellenistic and [[ Roman world | religion-in-ancient-rome ]], while beliefs in
the Egyptian god [[ Sarapis ]] were thoroughly Hellenized by the Ptolemaic
rulers of Egypt before the spread of his cult to Macedonia and the Aegean
region.[[ ^359 ]] The German historian [[ Johann Gustav Droysen ]] argued that
the conquests of Alexander the Great and creation of the Hellenistic world
allowed for the growth and
[[ establishment of Christianity | history-of-christianity ]] in the
[[ Roman era ]].[[ ^360 ]]

