[[ ^15 ]]: Written evidence about Macedonian governmental institutions made before [[ Philip II of Macedon ]]'s reign is both rare and non-Macedonian in origin. The main sources of early Macedonian historiography are the works of [[ Herodotus ]], [[ Thucydides ]], [[ Diodorus Siculus ]], and [[ Justin | justin-historian ]]. Contemporary accounts given by those such as [[ Demosthenes ]] were often hostile and unreliable; even [[ Aristotle ]], who lived in Macedonia, provides us with terse accounts of its governing institutions. [[ Polybius ]] was a contemporary historian who wrote about Macedonia; later historians include [[ Livy ]], [[ Quintus Curtius Rufus ]], [[ Plutarch ]], and [[ Arrian ]]. The works of these historians affirm Macedonia's [[ hereditary monarchy ]] and basic institutions, yet it remains unclear if there was an established [[ constitution ]] for Macedonian government. See: King 2010, pp. 373–374. However, [[ N. G. L. Hammond ]] and [[ F. W. Walbank ]] write with apparent certainty and conviction when describing the Macedonian constitutional government restricting [[ the king | kings-of-macedon ]] and involving a [[ popular assembly ]] of the army. See: Hammond & Walbank 2001, pp. 12–13. The main textual primary sources for the organization of [[ Macedonia's military | ancient-macedonian-army ]] as it existed under Alexander the Great include Arrian, Curtis, Diodorus, and Plutarch; modern historians rely mostly on Polybius and [[ Livy ]] for understanding detailed aspects of [[ the Antigonid-period military | antigonid-macedonian-army ]]. On this, Sekunda 2010, pp. 446–447 writes: "\... to this we can add the evidence provided by two magnificent archaeological monuments, the '[[ Alexander Sarcophagus ]]' in particular and the '[[ Alexander Mosaic ]]'\... In the case of the [[ Antigonid army | antigonid-macedonian-army ]] \... valuable additional details are occasionally supplied by [[ Diodorus | diodorus-siculus ]] and [[ Plutarch ]], and by a series of inscriptions preserving sections of two sets of army regulations issued by [[ Philip V | philip-v-of-macedon ]]."

[[ ^211 ]]: King 2010, p. 374; see also Errington 1990, pp. 220–221 for further details.

[[ ^16 ]]: King 2010, p. 374; for an argument about the [[ absolutism | absolute-monarchy ]] of the Macedonian monarchy, see Errington 1990, pp. 220–222. However, [[ N. G. L. Hammond ]] and [[ F. W. Walbank ]] write with apparent certainty and conviction when describing the Macedonian constitutional government restricting [[ the king | kings-of-macedon ]] and involving a [[ popular assembly ]] of the army. Hammond & Walbank 2001, pp. 12–13.

[[ ^17 ]]: King 2010, p. 375. In 1931 Friedrich Granier was the first to propose that by the time of Philip II's reign, Macedonia had a constitutional government with laws that delegated rights and [[ customary privileges | uncodified-constitution ]] to certain groups, especially to its citizen soldiers, although the majority of evidence for the army's alleged right to [[ appoint a new king | elective-monarchy ]] and judge cases of [[ treason ]] stems from the reign of [[ Alexander III of Macedon ]]. See Granier 1931, pp. 4–28, 48–57 and King 2010, pp. 374–375. [[ Pietro De Francisci ]] was the first to refute Granier's ideas and advance the theory that the Macedonian government was an [[ autocracy ]] ruled by the whim of the monarch, although this issue of kingship and governance is still unresolved in academia. See: de Francisci 1948, pp. 345–435 as well as King 2010, p. 375 and Errington 1990, p. 220 for further details.

