[[ ^24 ]]: Amemiya 2007, pp. 11–12: under [[ Antipater ]]'s oligarchy, the lower value in terms of property for acceptable members of the oligarchy was 2,000 //[[ drachma | ancient-drachma ]]//. [[ Athenian democracy ]] was restored briefly after Antipater's death in 319 BC, yet his son [[ Cassander ]] reconquered the city, which came under the regency of [[ Demetrius of Phalerum ]]. Demetrius lowered the property limit for oligarchic members to 1,000 //drachma//, yet by 307 BC he was exiled from the city and [[ direct democracy ]] was restored. [[ Demetrius I of Macedon ]] reconquered Athens in 295 BC, yet democracy was once again restored in 287 BC with the aid of [[ Ptolemy I of Egypt ]]. [[ Antigonus II Gonatas ]], son of Demetrius I, reconquered Athens in 260 BC, followed by a succession of Macedonian kings ruling over Athens until the [[ Roman Republic ]] conquered both Macedonia and then [[ mainland Greece ]] by 146 BC.

