[[ ^48 ]]: Worthington 2012, p. 319. As [[ pharaoh ]] of the Egyptians, he was already titled [[ Son of Ra | ra ]] and considered the living incarnation of [[ Horus ]] by his Egyptian subjects (a belief that the [[ Ptolemaic successors | ptolemaic-kingdom ]] of Alexander would foster for [[ their own dynasty in Egypt | ptolemaic-dynasty ]]). See: Worthington 2014, p. 180 and Sansone 2017, p. 228 for details.

[[ ^49 ]]: Worthington 2012, p. 319; Worthington 2014, pp. 180–183. After the priest and [[ Oracle | oracle-of-ammon ]] of [[ Zeus Ammon ]] at the [[ Siwa Oasis ]] convinced him that Philip II was merely his mortal father and Zeus his actual father, Alexander began styling himself as the 'Son of Zeus', which brought him into contention with some of his Greek subjects who adamantly believed that living men could not be immortals. See Worthington 2012, p. 319 and Worthington 2014, pp. 182–183 for details.

